XX. 🌐 FEDERATION — MIGRATION HANDSHAKE

A Citadel changing its Constitution can affect its federation partners.

Therefore:

CITADEL A
Cₙ → Cₙ₊₁
       │
       ↓
FEDERATION IMPACT
       │
       ├── COMPATIBLE
       ├── CONDITIONAL
       └── INCOMPATIBLE

Where required:

CITADEL A
 ↓
MIGRATION NOTICE
 ↓
FEDERATIVE COMPATIBILITY CHECK
 ↓
CITADEL B
 ↓
ACKNOWLEDGEMENT / CHALLENGE

But again:

> A federation partner may challenge a transition; it does not automatically possess authority to rewrite another Citadel's Constitution.




---

XXI. 🦆 FOOL — FEDERATED MIGRATION CHALLENGE

FOOL asks:

Did the Citadel possess amendment authority?

Was the migration correctly authorized?

Was the effective time valid?

Were external parties notified where required?

Did any delegation cross the transition boundary?

Did the migration create unexpected authority?

Did the migration invalidate a federative obligation?

Was rollback itself constitutionally valid?

And:

> Can you prove that the new constitutional state is the state you claim it is?




---

XXII. 🔒 R6.99 CANONICAL INVARIANTS

MIG-I01

Constitutional migration SHALL create an identifiable state transition.

MIG-I02

Migration SHALL NOT erase the source constitutional state.

MIG-I03

Every material migration SHALL possess an explicit authority basis.

MIG-I04

Every material migration SHALL possess evidence.

MIG-I05

Every material migration SHALL be watermarkable.

MIG-I06

The transition boundary SHALL possess deterministic temporal semantics.

MIG-I07

Historical actions SHALL remain governed by the constitutional state applicable at the relevant execution time.

MIG-I08

Migration SHALL NOT silently expand existing authority.

MIG-I09

Migration SHALL explicitly evaluate affected delegations.

MIG-I10

Migration SHALL explicitly evaluate affected ELIXERS where applicable.

MIG-I11

Operational compatibility SHALL be distinguished from constitutional validity.

MIG-I12

Federative compatibility SHALL be explicitly evaluated where required.

MIG-I13

BRAINPOWER SHALL NOT independently authorize migration.

MIG-I14

FOOL SHALL be able to challenge migration validity.

MIG-I15

TRIAS SHALL determine migration validity.

MIG-I16

Harbor SHALL be capable of constraining sensitive operations during transition where constitutionally or operationally required.

MIG-I17

A failed migration SHALL remain historically identifiable.

MIG-I18

Rollback SHALL NOT erase the fact that the target state was activated or attempted.

MIG-I19

Rollback SHALL itself constitute a governed state transition.

MIG-I20

An independent verifier SHALL be able to reconstruct the complete constitutional migration history.


