And now the next pressure point appears naturally:

> What happens when different Citadels are simultaneously on different constitutional versions and need to perform one shared action?



That is the point where versioning, delegation, federation, compatibility and authority finally collide in one operation.

🦆☄️🌱 R7.00 — HORTUS FEDERATED CROSS-VERSION CONSENSUS & CONSTITUTIONAL INTEROPERABILITY

The Federation must now learn how to act together without pretending that all Citadels are constitutionally identical.

☄️🌱 R7.00 — HORTUS FEDERATED CROSS-VERSION CONSENSUS & CONSTITUTIONAL INTEROPERABILITY

R6.99 is frozen.

We have reached a genuine architectural boundary.

R6 established that governance can be delegated, challenged, versioned, migrated, revoked, and replayed.

R7.00 addresses the next problem:

> How can independently governed Citadels cooperate when they do not necessarily share the same constitutional version?



The answer is not forced uniformity.

It is:

PROVEN INTEROPERABILITY WITHOUT SOVEREIGNTY COLLAPSE.


---

I. 🌐 THE FEDERATION PROBLEM

Imagine:

CITADEL A
Constitution C₇
     │
     │
     ├──────── FEDERATION ────────┐
     │                            │
CITADEL B                     CITADEL C
Constitution C₈              Constitution C₇

A common mistake would be:

FEDERATION
    ↓
ONE CONSTITUTION
    ↓
EVERYONE MUST OBEY IT

PALACO rejects this.

Instead:

CITADEL A
    │
    ├── owns its constitutional state
    │
    └── participates in federation

CITADEL B
    │
    ├── owns its constitutional state
    │
    └── participates in federation

Therefore:

> Federation creates interoperable obligations, not automatic constitutional sovereignty over its members.




---

II. 🔱 THREE LAYERS OF VALIDITY

R7.00 introduces a critical distinction.

For a federated action:

ACTION
 │
 ├── LOCAL VALIDITY
 │
 ├── FEDERATIVE VALIDITY
 │
 └── CROSS-VERSION COMPATIBILITY

These are separate questions.

Local validity

> Was this action authorized under the originating Citadel's Constitution?



Federative validity

> Was the action permitted under the applicable federation agreement?



Compatibility

> Can the participating constitutional states legally and operationally cooperate for this action?



A positive answer to one does not automatically produce a positive answer to the others.


---

III. 🌱 Z.A.N.D. — NO IMPLIED COMPATIBILITY

The first gate remains:

ASSUMPTION = NONE

Therefore:

C₇ + C₈

does not imply:

COMPATIBLE

Compatibility must be established.

Questions:

WHAT IS THE SHARED ACTION?

WHICH CITADELS PARTICIPATE?

WHICH CONSTITUTIONAL STATES APPLY?

WHICH FEDERATIVE RULES APPLY?

WHICH AUTHORITIES ARE INVOLVED?

WHICH DELEGATIONS ARE INVOLVED?

WHAT HAPPENS IF THEIR RULES DIFFER?

WHO RESOLVES CONFLICT?

WHAT EVIDENCE PROVES THE AGREEMENT?

If unresolved:

INTEROPERABILITY = UNPROVEN


---

IV. 🧬 THE INTEROPERABILITY CONTRACT

R7.00 introduces:

FEDERATED INTEROPERABILITY CONTRACT

Conceptually:

InteroperabilityContract {
    contract_id

    participants

    local_constitution_versions

    shared_action_scope

    required_capabilities

    authority_requirements

    consent_requirements

    delegation_requirements

    temporal_bounds

    conflict_rules

    evidence_requirements

    revocation_rules

    termination_conditions

    provenance

    watermark
}

This is not a replacement Constitution.

It is a bounded bridge between constitutional systems.


---

V. ⚖️ FEDERATION ≠ SOVEREIGNTY

The central invariant:

> Federation membership SHALL NOT confer constitutional authority over another Citadel.



Therefore:

A
 │
 └── federates with B

does not mean:

A
 ↓
may govern
 ↓
B

Nor:

B
 ↓
may govern
 ↓
A

unless a separate, explicit and proven authority relationship exists.

Federation is a relationship.

Authority remains bounded.


---

VI. 🧠 BRAINPOWER — CROSS-VERSION ANALYSIS

BRAINPOWER receives:

C₇
+
C₈
+
FEDERATION CONTRACT

and computes a candidate interoperability model.

For example:

C₇ permits:
DOCUMENT_VERIFICATION

C₈ permits:
DOCUMENT_VERIFICATION

Federation contract requires:
EVIDENCE + WATERMARK + NO_EXTERNAL_ACTION

BRAINPOWER can conclude:

CANDIDATE:
INTEROPERABLE

But this remains:

ANALYSIS

not:

AUTHORIZATION

The chain remains:

BRAINPOWER
 ↓
INTERPRETATION
 ↓
EVIDENCE
 ↓
TRIAS
 ↓
DETERMINATION


---

VII. 🦆 FOOL — CROSS-VERSION CHALLENGE

FOOL attacks the bridge.

It asks:

> Do both Citadels actually possess the claimed authority?



> Are the constitutional versions correctly identified?



> Does the federation contract actually permit this action?



> Does one Citadel assume authority that belongs only to the other?



> Did either party rely on an obsolete constitutional version?



> Does the action exceed the narrowest applicable scope?



And the key question:

> If C₇ and C₈ disagree, which rule governs this particular operation — and why?




---

VIII. 🧭 THE NARROWEST VALID BOUNDARY

R7.00 introduces a powerful operational principle:

> Cross-Citadel authority SHALL NOT exceed the intersection of independently proven permissions.



Conceptually:

AUTHORITY A
      ∩
AUTHORITY B
      ∩
FEDERATION SCOPE
      ∩
ACTION SCOPE
      =
VALID FEDERATED SCOPE

Example:

A permits:
READ + ANALYZE + EXPORT

B permits:
READ + ANALYZE

Federation permits:
READ + ANALYZE

Result:

READ + ANALYZE

Not:

READ + ANALYZE + EXPORT

This prevents capability inflation at federation boundaries.


---

IX. 💧 WATERMARKED FEDERATED AUTHORITY

A shared action receives a multi-party lineage:

W_A
LOCAL AUTHORITY A

W_B
LOCAL AUTHORITY B

W_F
FEDERATIVE AGREEMENT

W_S
SHARED SCOPE

W_T
TEMPORAL VALIDITY

W_X
CROSS-VERSION DETERMINATION

W_ACT
ACTION

The result:

FEDERATED ACTION
=
MULTI-PROVENANCE ACTION

No single watermark should obscure the contributing authorities.


---

