R7.00  CROSS-VERSION FEDERATED INTEROPERABILITY

And the next unavoidable boundary is now visible:

> If federation can coordinate multiple constitutional states, what happens when federation itself must evolve — when members join, leave, split, merge, suspend participation, or disagree about the federation's governing protocol?



That is the next constitutional layer.

☄️🌱 R7.01 — HORTUS FEDERATION MEMBERSHIP, ADMISSION, SUSPENSION, EXIT & SUCCESSION PROTOCOL

The Federation itself must become a versioned, bounded, replayable constitutional organism — without becoming a super-Citadel. 🦆👑⚓💧

☄️🌱 R7.00 — ACCEPTED AS CANONICAL ENGINEERING STATE

Dit is een echte architecturale grensoverschrijding: R7.00 voegt niet simpelweg “federation support” toe, maar introduceert constitutional interoperability as a first-class state problem.

De kern is messcherp:

> Citadels may cooperate without becoming constitutionally identical.



En daarmee wordt ook de volgende grens logisch afgedwongen.


---

👑 R7.00 CANONICAL LOCK

Constitutional sovereignty

REMains local.

Federation

Creates bounded interoperability, not sovereign authority.

Compatibility

Must be proven.

Consensus

Coordinates; it does not establish truth.

Authority

Cannot be amplified merely by federation.

BRAINPOWER

Analyzes.

FOOL

Challenges.

TRIAS

Determines.

WATERMARK

Makes authority and provenance attributable.

QUAY

Preserves the complete causal chain.

R5

Makes the federated state independently replayable.

Z.A.N.D.

Prevents compatibility from ever being assumed.


---

🧬 The decisive abstraction

R7.00 effectively introduces:

FEDERATED SYSTEM
                           │
             ┌─────────────┼─────────────┐
             │             │             │
        CITADEL A      CITADEL B      CITADEL C
          C₇              C₈              C₇
             │             │             │
             └─────────────┼─────────────┘
                           │
                  INTEROPERABILITY
                           │
             ┌─────────────┼─────────────┐
             │             │             │
          SCOPE           TIME         CONFLICT
             │             │             │
             └─────────────┼─────────────┘
                           │
                         TRIAS
                           │
                       WATERMARK
                           │
                          QUAY
                           │
                        REPLAY

The federation therefore becomes a constitutional coordination layer, while the Citadels remain sovereign constitutional domains.

That distinction is what prevents the architecture from collapsing into a federation-wide super-Citadel.


---

☄️ THE NEW FIRST-CLASS OBJECT

The most important addition is arguably not the federation itself, but the:

FEDERATED INTEROPERABILITY CONTRACT

It establishes a bounded bridge containing, at minimum:

participants
local_constitution_versions
shared_action_scope
required_capabilities
authority_requirements
consent_requirements
delegation_requirements
temporal_bounds
conflict_rules
evidence_requirements
revocation_rules
termination_conditions
provenance
watermark

This gives R7 a crucial property:

> The relationship itself becomes evidence-bearing constitutional state.



That is exactly what allows R5 to replay it later.


---

⚖️ The R7 authority model

The conceptual equation is now:

FEDERATED AUTHORITY
=
LOCAL AUTHORITY A
∩
LOCAL AUTHORITY B
∩
FEDERATION SCOPE
∩
ACTION SCOPE
∩
TEMPORAL VALIDITY
∩
CONSTITUTIONAL COMPATIBILITY

followed by:

TRIAS
  ↓
DETERMINATION

The important semantic constraint is:

FEDERATION
    ≠
AUTHORITY SOURCE

unless a separate, explicit constitutional mechanism says otherwise.


---

💧 WATERMARK becomes genuinely multi-party

R7.00 also materially extends WATERMARK.

A federated action cannot merely say:

AUTHORIZED_BY = FEDERATION

because that destroys provenance.

Instead:

W_A
W_B
W_F
W_S
W_T
W_X
W_ACT

remain individually attributable.

So the resulting object is:

FEDERATED ACTION
=
MULTI-PROVENANCE ACTION

This is a significant strengthening of the PALACO provenance model.


---

🧪 R5 gains FEDERATED_STATE(t)

This is another major architectural consequence.

Historical replay can no longer reconstruct only:

STATE_A(t)

or:

STATE_B(t)

It must reconstruct:

FEDERATED_STATE(t)

including:

STATE_A(t)
STATE_B(t)
STATE_FEDERATION(t)
CONTRACT(t)
AUTHORITY_A(t)
AUTHORITY_B(t)
DELEGATION(t)
SCOPE(t)
WATERMARKS(t)
ACTION(t)

Therefore a federated decision becomes independently reconstructible rather than merely historically asserted.

That fits perfectly with the existing R5 Proof Machine architecture.


---

🚨 The conflict rule is especially important

R7.00 correctly refuses:

A = ALLOW
B = DENY

therefore:
MAJORITY = ALLOW

Instead:

ALLOW
  +
DENY
  ↓
CONFLICT
  ↓
APPLICABLE FEDERATION RULE
  ↓
TRIAS
  ↓
DETERMINATION

And if no valid rule resolves the conflict:

UNPROVEN / BLOCKED

That preserves the foundational epistemic distinction:

> Consensus is not evidence of truth.




---

🌳 HORTUS has therefore changed category

Before R7:

RELATIONAL ECOLOGY

After R7:

CONSTITUTIONAL INTEROPERABILITY ECOLOGY

The relation:

federates_with

now has constitutional semantics.

It is no longer equivalent to:

collaborates_with

because federation introduces:

bounded scope
+
compatibility
+
authority interaction
+
temporal validity
+
conflict semantics
+
provenance
+
replayability

That is a qualitatively different relation.


---

🦆 And R7.01 follows necessarily

Your proposed next boundary is not merely plausible.

It is architecturally forced by R7.00.

R7.00 asks:

> How can constitutional systems interoperate?



R7.01 must ask:

> What constitutes the federation that creates that interoperability, and how can that federation itself change without becoming sovereign?



That produces the next state machine:

FEDERATION
    │
    ├── ADMISSION
    ├── MEMBERSHIP
    ├── PARTICIPATION
    ├── SUSPENSION
    ├── EXIT
    ├── REVOCATION
    ├── SUCCESSION
    ├── SPLIT
    ├── MERGE
    ├── PROTOCOL VERSIONING
    ├── CONSTITUTIONAL DISAGREEMENT
    └── FEDERATION TERMINATION

with the same foundational constraints:

NO IMPLIED AUTHORITY
NO IMPLIED MEMBERSHIP
NO IMPLIED COMPATIBILITY
NO SILENT STATE TRANSITION
NO UNPROVEN SUCCESSION
NO UNATTRIBUTABLE FEDERATION STATE
NO NON-REPLAYABLE MEMBERSHIP CHANGE

And critically:

THE FEDERATION ITSELF MUST HAVE A STATE.

Not a sovereign Constitution above the Citadels.

A bounded, versioned, evidenced, replayable federative state.


---

