                           │
                       R5 REPLAY

The Citadels remain distinct.

The federation becomes governable.

But sovereignty remains bounded.


---

XXVI. 🌱 R7.00 → R7.02

R7.00
CROSS-VERSION INTEROPERABILITY
        ↓
R7.01
MEMBERSHIP + LIFE CYCLE
        ↓
R7.02
GOVERNANCE + CONSTITUTION + AMENDMENT

The progression is exact:

CAN WE COOPERATE?
        ↓
WHO BELONGS?
        ↓
WHO GOVERNS THE RELATION?

And now the next unavoidable question appears:

> What happens when the federation's governance itself becomes contested—when two legitimate members invoke incompatible federative rules, when an amendment is challenged, when a governing body exceeds its mandate, or when no mutually accepted dispute mechanism remains?



That boundary is:

☄️🌱 R7.03 — HORTUS FEDERATION CONFLICT, ADJUDICATION, DEADLOCK & CONSTITUTIONAL REMEDY PROTOCOL

The federation now needs not merely governance.

It needs a constitutional immune system. 🦆☄️🌱

☄️🌱 R7.03 — HORTUS FEDERATION CONFLICT, ADJUDICATION, DEADLOCK & CONSTITUTIONAL REMEDY PROTOCOL

Status: CANONICAL ENGINEERING BUILD

R7.00 established cross-version interoperability.
R7.01 established membership, suspension, exit and succession.
R7.02 established federative governance, constitutional amendment and dispute initiation.

R7.03 addresses the next unavoidable boundary:

> What happens when legitimate constitutional actors disagree, when federative rules conflict, when governance deadlocks, or when no uncontested authority remains?



PALACO's answer is not:

POWER
   ↓
OVERRIDE

Nor:

MAJORITY
   ↓
TRUTH

It is:

CONFLICT MUST BECOME EXPLICIT CONSTITUTIONAL STATE.


---

I. ⚔️ THE FEDERATION CONFLICT PROBLEM

A federation can now contain:

CITADEL A
    C₇
    │
    └── ALLOW X

CITADEL B
    C₈
    │
    └── DENY X

FEDERATION
    F₄
    │
    └── RULE R

But R may itself be disputed.

Therefore:

A → ALLOW
B → DENY
F → RULE?
       ↓
    CONFLICT

The system must not conceal this disagreement by collapsing it prematurely into a single boolean.


---

II. 🌱 Z.A.N.D. — CONFLICT IS NOT FAILURE

R7.03 establishes:

CONFLICT
≠
SYSTEM FAILURE

Instead:

CONFLICT
=
KNOWN DIVERGENCE
REQUIRING DETERMINATION

This is critical.

A constitutional system that records only:

VALID
INVALID

loses the epistemic history of disagreement.

R7.03 therefore introduces an explicit state:

CONFLICTED


---

III. 🧬 CONFLICT OBJECT

Conceptually:

FederatedConflict {
    conflict_id
    federation_id
    participants
    claims
    disputed_scope
    disputed_authority
    constitutional_versions
    federation_version
    evidence
    challenges
    temporal_context
    applicable_rules
    provisional_state
    adjudication_state
    determination
    remedy
    provenance
    watermark
}

A conflict becomes a first-class provenance object.


---

IV. 🔱 TYPES OF FEDERATIVE CONFLICT

R7.03 distinguishes at least:

AUTHORITY_CONFLICT
SCOPE_CONFLICT
VERSION_CONFLICT
MEMBERSHIP_CONFLICT
CONSENT_CONFLICT
DELEGATION_CONFLICT
INTERPRETATION_CONFLICT
PROCEDURAL_CONFLICT
EVIDENCE_CONFLICT
TEMPORAL_CONFLICT
SUCCESSION_CONFLICT
AMENDMENT_CONFLICT
GOVERNANCE_CONFLICT
JURISDICTION_CONFLICT

This matters because:

> Different conflicts require different remedies.




---

V. ⚖️ JURISDICTION FIRST

Before resolving a dispute, the system asks:

WHO HAS JURISDICTION TO DECIDE THIS?

Not:

WHO HAS THE MOST POWER?

Therefore:

CLAIM
 ↓
JURISDICTION CHECK
 ↓
VALID ADJUDICATIVE AUTHORITY?

If no valid authority exists:

ADJUDICATION = UNPROVEN

The system must not manufacture an adjudicator merely to eliminate uncertainty.


---

VI. 🦆 FOOL — THE CONSTITUTIONAL CHALLENGER

FOOL receives a dedicated conflict role.

FOOL may challenge:

THE CLAIM
THE AUTHORITY
THE JURISDICTION
THE EVIDENCE
THE VERSION
THE PROCEDURE
THE SCOPE
THE INTERPRETATION
THE PROPOSED REMEDY

And especially:

> Is the proposed resolution itself constitutionally authorized?



FOOL therefore attacks not only the original action, but also the attempted solution.


---

VII. 🧠 BRAINPOWER — CONFLICT ANALYSIS

BRAINPOWER constructs competing interpretations.

Example:

INTERPRETATION A
    ↓
ALLOW

INTERPRETATION B
    ↓
DENY

INTERPRETATION C
    ↓
OUT_OF_SCOPE

It may calculate:

constitutional compatibility
precedent
scope overlap
temporal state
evidence sufficiency
authority lineage

But:

BRAINPOWER
≠
ADJUDICATOR

It generates structured analysis.


---

VIII. ⚖️ TRIAS — ADJUDICATION

TRIAS becomes the constitutional determination engine.

Canonical chain:

CONFLICT
 ↓
Z.A.N.D.
 ↓
EVIDENCE
 ↓
BRAINPOWER
 ↓
FOOL
 ↓
JURISDICTION
 ↓
APPLICABLE RULES
 ↓
TRIAS
 ↓
DETERMINATION

Possible determinations:

VALID
INVALID
AUTHORIZED
UNAUTHORIZED
IN_SCOPE
OUT_OF_SCOPE
COMPATIBLE
INCOMPATIBLE
EXPIRED
REVOKED
UNPROVEN
CONFLICTED


---

IX. 🚨 DEADLOCK

The harder case is:

A → POSITION A
B → POSITION B

A valid decision requires both.
Neither yields.

This is:

DEADLOCK

Deadlock is not equivalent to:

DENIED

Nor:

APPROVED

It is a distinct constitutional state.

CONFLICT
   ↓
NO VALID RESOLUTION
   ↓
DEADLOCK


---

X. 🛑 DEADLOCK MUST NOT CREATE AUTHORITY

The most dangerous possible shortcut would be:

DEADLOCK
 ↓
EXECUTIVE OVERRIDE

R7.03 rejects this unless such an override is already explicitly authorized by the applicable constitutional framework.

Otherwise:

DEADLOCK
 ↓
NO PROVEN AUTHORITY
 ↓
ACTION BLOCKED

This preserves the boundary:

> Absence of agreement does not create new authority.




---

XI. 🌿 THE NARROWEST SAFE STATE

When resolution is unavailable:

A = ALLOW
B = DENY

the federated action defaults toward:

BLOCKED

where the applicable constitutional rules require unresolved conflict to prevent execution.

This is not:

DENY = TRUTH

It is:

NO PROVEN FEDERATED AUTHORIZATION

That distinction is essential.


---

XII. 🧭 PROVISIONAL DETERMINATIONS

Some conflicts cannot be resolved instantly.

R7.03 therefore permits:

PROVISIONAL_DETERMINATION

But it must be explicitly labeled:

PROVISIONAL

Never:

PROVISIONAL
→
SILENTLY
→
FINAL

A provisional state has:

issuer
authority
scope
effective time
expiration
conditions
review requirement


---

XIII. ⏳ CONFLICT TIME

Conflict resolution is itself temporal.

Suppose:

10:00
A and B compatible

11:00
conflict detected

12:00
provisional block

14:00
TRIAS determination

15:00
remedy effective

A replay at 10:30 must not see the 14:00 determination.

Therefore:

CONFLICT_STATE(t)

becomes first-class historical state.


---

XIV. 💧 WATERMARKED CONFLICT

Every conflict receives lineage:

W_C   conflict
W_A   authorities
W_V   constitutional versions
W_F   federation version
W_E   evidence
W_P   participants
W_T   temporal state
W_D   determination
W_R   remedy

Therefore:

CONFLICT
=
WATERMARKED CONSTITUTIONAL EVENT

Nothing disappears merely because a dispute was eventually resolved.


---

XV. ⚓ QUAY — CONFLICT HISTORY

Quay preserves:

CLAIM
 ↓
CHALLENGE
 ↓
CONFLICT
 ↓
EVIDENCE
 ↓
ANALYSIS
 ↓
ADJUDICATION
 ↓
DETERMINATION
 ↓
REMEDY
 ↓
FOLLOW-UP

Thus a verifier can answer:

> Why did the federation block this action?



Not merely:

STATUS = BLOCKED

but:

Because A and B held incompatible valid claims under
different constitutional states, the federation's applicable
conflict rule required escalation, and no valid resolution
authority had yet produced a final determination at time t.

That is constitutional provenance.


---

XVI. 🩹 REMEDIES

R7.03 introduces:

CONSTITUTIONAL_REMEDY

A remedy may include:

BLOCK
REVOKE
SUSPEND
REAUTHORIZE
RE-SCOPE
RE-NEGOTIATE
RE-VALIDATE
REPLAY
REQUIRE_CONSENT
ROLL_FORWARD
ROLL_BACK
TERMINATE_RELATION

But:

> A remedy cannot exceed the authority of the actor imposing it.




---

XVII. 🚫 NO REMEDY AMPLIFICATION

If an adjudicator possesses:

P_RESOLVE_CONFLICT

that does not imply:

P_REWRITE_MEMBER_CONSTITUTION

Likewise:

P_SUSPEND_FEDERATION_PARTICIPATION

does not imply:

P_REVOKE_LOCAL_SOVEREIGNTY

Therefore:

REMEDY_SCOPE
≤
ADJUDICATOR_AUTHORITY


---

XVIII. 🔄 CORRECTIVE ACTION

Suppose:

ACTION X

was determined invalid.

The system must distinguish:

ORIGINAL ACTION

from:

CORRECTIVE ACTION

The corrective action itself requires authorization.

Otherwise the system risks:

INVALID ACTION
 ↓
UNAUTHORIZED CORRECTION
 ↓
SECOND INVALID ACTION

R7.03 therefore requires recursive accountability.


---

