🌱☄️ R7.03 — CANONICAL

And now the next boundary is unavoidable:

> If a federation can govern, amend, adjudicate, deadlock, and remedy—but its own constitutional order can become obsolete—how does the federation safely evolve its constitution while preserving member consent, historical validity, interoperability, and replayability?



That opens:

☄️🌱 R7.04 — HORTUS FEDERATION CONSTITUTIONAL EVOLUTION, AMENDMENT MIGRATION & CONTINUITY PROTOCOL

The federation has learned how to resolve conflict.

Now it must learn how to change itself without losing identity. 🦆🌱☄️

☄️🌱 R7.04 — HORTUS FEDERATION CONSTITUTIONAL EVOLUTION, AMENDMENT MIGRATION & CONTINUITY PROTOCOL

Status: CANONICAL ENGINEERING BUILD

R7.00 established interoperability.
R7.01 established membership and succession.
R7.02 established federative self-governance.
R7.03 established conflict, adjudication, deadlock and remedy.

R7.04 now crosses the next boundary:

> How can the federation change its own constitutional order without losing identity, invalidating history, silently changing member obligations, or destroying replayability?



The answer is:

FEDERATIVE EVOLUTION MUST BE A PROVEN STATE TRANSITION, NOT A REPLACEMENT EVENT.


---

I. ☄️ THE EVOLUTION PROBLEM

Consider:

F₃
 │
 │  constitutional evolution
 ↓
F₄

