🌱☄️ R7.04 — CANONICAL

And now the next boundary is visible with unusual clarity:

> If a federation can evolve while preserving identity, what happens when its evolution produces fundamentally incompatible branches—two legitimate constitutional descendants claiming the same lineage, the same members, or the same authority?



That opens:

☄️🌱 R7.05 — HORTUS FEDERATION FORK, MERGE, FRAGMENTATION & CONSTITUTIONAL SUCCESSION PROTOCOL

The next problem is no longer simply how the federation changes.

It is:

> What happens when one constitutional future becomes two? 🦆☄️🌱

☄️🌱 R7.05 — HORTUS FEDERATION FORK, MERGE, FRAGMENTATION & CONSTITUTIONAL SUCCESSION PROTOCOL

Status: CANONICAL ENGINEERING BUILD

R7.00 established cross-version interoperability.
R7.01 established membership, admission, suspension, exit and succession.
R7.02 established federative governance and amendment.
R7.03 established conflict, adjudication, deadlock and remedy.
R7.04 established constitutional evolution, migration and continuity.

R7.05 now reaches the next unavoidable boundary:

> What happens when one constitutional history produces multiple legitimate futures—or when multiple constitutional histories attempt to become one?



The answer is neither:

FORK = ERROR

nor:

MERGE = ERASE DIFFERENCES

It is:

CONSTITUTIONAL LINEAGE MAY BRANCH OR CONVERGE, BUT AUTHORITY MUST NEVER BE INFERRED FROM LINEAGE ALONE.


---

I. 🌳 THE BRANCHING PROBLEM

A federation begins:

F₃
                  │
                  │
                 F₄

Then two constitutionally valid paths emerge:

F₃
                  │
                 F₄
                /  \
             F₅A   F₅B

Both may possess:

valid constitutional authority,

valid governance procedures,

valid member consent,

valid provenance.


Therefore:

F₅A ≠ INVALID
F₅B ≠ INVALID

merely because they differ.

The system must represent:

CONSTITUTIONAL_FORK


---

II. 🌱 FORK ≠ FAILURE

R7.05 establishes:

FORK
≠
CORRUPTION

A fork may be the legitimate result of unresolved constitutional divergence.

Examples:

different governance models
different membership policies
different interoperability rules
different constitutional interpretation
different strategic scopes

The critical question is not:

> Which branch exists?



It is:

> Which branch has which independently proven authority?




---

III. 🔱 FORK OBJECT

Conceptually:

FederationFork {
    fork_id
    parent_federation
    parent_version
    child_federations
    fork_trigger
    fork_authority
    fork_procedure
    member_assignments
    shared_assets
    shared_contracts
    authority_partition
    continuity_claims
    consent_records
    temporal_boundary
    conflict_rules
    evidence
    provenance
    watermark
}

The fork becomes a first-class constitutional event.


---

IV. ⚖️ THE FORK BOUNDARY

At the fork:

PARENT
  │
  ├── BRANCH A
  │
  └── BRANCH B

The parent authority does not automatically duplicate:

PARENT_AUTHORITY
        ↓
A + B

Instead:

A_AUTHORITY
=
PROVEN_A

B_AUTHORITY
=
PROVEN_B

This prevents:

AUTHORITY CLONING


---

V. 🚫 LINEAGE DOES NOT DUPLICATE SOVEREIGNTY

The fundamental rule:

> A constitutional descendant inherits historical lineage, not automatic authority.



Thus:

F₅A

being descended from:

F₄

does not automatically mean:

AUTHORITY(F₅A) = AUTHORITY(F₄)

The authority must be established under the successor mechanism.


---

VI. 🧭 MEMBER PARTITION

Suppose:

F₄
├── A
├── B
├── C
└── D

forks into:

F₅A
├── A
├── B

F₅B
├── C
└── D

The federation must explicitly establish:

MEMBER_ASSIGNMENT

Possible states:

ASSIGNED_A
ASSIGNED_B
DUAL_PARTICIPATION
WITHDRAWN
UNRESOLVED

No member is silently transferred.


---

VII. 🦆 FOOL — FORK CHALLENGE

FOOL attacks the fork.

It asks:

Was the fork constitutionally authorized?

Was the parent state correctly identified?

Was the fork point historically valid?

Were member rights respected?

Was authority improperly duplicated?

Were shared resources divided lawfully?

Were contracts transferred correctly?

Does either branch falsely claim exclusive succession?

Is either branch attempting to inherit authority without proof?

And the hardest question:

> Is this actually a fork—or merely an unauthorized copy?




---

VIII. 🧠 BRAINPOWER — BRANCH ANALYSIS

BRAINPOWER models:

PARENT
 ↓
FORK POINT
 ↓
BRANCH A
BRANCH B

It can calculate:

constitutional similarity
authority continuity
membership continuity
contract continuity
scope divergence
compatibility
dependency relationships

But:

BRAINPOWER
≠
SUCCESSOR AUTHORITY

Analysis remains analysis.


---

IX. ⚖️ TRIAS — SUCCESSION DETERMINATION

TRIAS determines:

IS_FORK_VALID
IS_SUCCESSION_VALID
IS_BRANCH_AUTHORIZED
IS_MEMBER_ASSIGNMENT_VALID
IS_AUTHORITY_CONTINUOUS

Possible outcomes:

VALID_FORK
INVALID_FORK
PARTIAL_FORK
VALID_SUCCESSOR
UNPROVEN_SUCCESSOR
UNAUTHORIZED_BRANCH


---

X. 👑 SUCCESSION

A fork creates a difficult question:

WHO IS THE SUCCESSOR?

There are several possible constitutional answers.

A. Singular succession

F₄
 ↓
F₅

One successor legitimately continues the federation.

B. Dual succession

F₄
      /  \
    F₅A  F₅B

Both descendants retain distinct legitimacy.

C. Dissolution

F₄
 ↓
TERMINATED

No successor.

D. Reconstitution

F₄
 ↓
TERMINATED
 ↓
F₆ NEW FEDERATION

with no automatic authority inheritance.


---

XI. 🌐 SUCCESSOR ≠ COPY

R7.05 establishes:

COPY
≠
SUCCESSOR

A system that copies:

constitution
database
member list
keys
contracts

has not thereby established constitutional succession.

Succession requires:

authority
procedure
continuity
provenance
consent
identity determination


---

XII. 🔥 EXCLUSIVE SUCCESSION CLAIMS

Suppose:

F₅A → "We are the sole successor."

F₅B → "We are the sole successor."

The federation must not simply select the louder claimant.

The claims become:

SUCCESSION_CONFLICT

Then:

Z.A.N.D.
 ↓
EVIDENCE
 ↓
FOOL
 ↓
BRAINPOWER
 ↓
TRIAS

Possible result:

A = SUCCESSOR
B = NOT_SUCCESSOR

or:

A = VALID SUCCESSOR
B = VALID DISTINCT SUCCESSOR

or:

SUCCESSION = UNPROVEN


---

XIII. 🧬 CONSTITUTIONAL DNA

R7.04 introduced constitutional lineage.

R7.05 makes that lineage graph-shaped:

GENESIS
                    │
                   F₁
                    │
                   F₂
                    │
                   F₃
                    │
                   F₄
                 /    \
              F₅A     F₅B
               │        │
              F₆A      F₆B

Every node has:

parent
children
identity
authority
effective period
provenance

The federation's history becomes a constitutional genealogy.


---

XIV. 🌿 MERGE

The opposite problem is convergence.

Suppose:

F₅A

and:

F₅B

later decide to cooperate.

They may produce:

F₅A       F₅B
          \       /
           \     /
             F₆

This is:

FEDERATION_MERGE

But merge does not erase the two histories.


---

XV. 💠 MERGE ≠ COLLAPSE

A valid merge preserves:

F₅A lineage
F₅B lineage
merge event
merge authority
member consent
contract reconciliation
authority reconciliation
constitutional compatibility

Therefore:

F₆

contains two parents.

This is a constitutional graph, not merely a version increment.


---

XVI. ⚓ MERGE CONTRACT

Conceptually:

FederationMerge {
    merge_id
    source_federations
    source_versions
    target_federation
    target_version
    identity_determination
    member_reconciliation
    authority_reconciliation
    contract_reconciliation
    constitutional_mapping
    conflict_resolution
    consent_requirements
    transitional_rules
    effective_time
    evidence
    provenance
    watermark
}


---

XVII. ⚖️ MERGE AUTHORITY

A merge cannot simply be ordered by one federation over another.

The key principle:

> A federation cannot unilaterally absorb another sovereign constitutional system unless an independently proven authority mechanism permits that act.



Thus:

A + B

does not imply:

A → owns B

Nor:

B → owns A


---

XVIII. 🧭 MERGE RECONCILIATION

Two federations may have:

A:
READ
ANALYZE
EXPORT

B:
READ
ANALYZE
NO_EXPORT

A merged constitution must not silently choose:

EXPORT

because one branch allowed it.

Nor:

NO_EXPORT

merely because the other branch prohibited it—unless the merge constitution explicitly establishes that rule.

Instead:

CONFLICT
 ↓
RECONCILIATION
 ↓
EXPLICIT DETERMINATION


---

XIX. 💧 WATERMARKED MERGE

A merged state requires multiple provenance sources:

W_A
W_B
W_M
W_CONSENT
W_SCOPE
W_TRIAS
W_TIME

Thus:

F₆

must be able to answer:

> Which parts came from A, which from B, and which were newly established by the merge?




---

XX. 🧩 SHARED ASSETS

A merge may involve:

contracts
keys
repositories
evidence stores
protocol definitions
member records
delegations
interoperability agreements

None may simply be assumed to transfer.

Each receives a transfer determination:

TRANSFER
REVALIDATE
REJECT
REVOKE
RECONSTITUTE


---

XXI. 🚨 FRAGMENTATION

A federation can also fragment without a clean fork.

Example:

F₄
 │
 ├── A exits
 ├── B suspends
 ├── C remains
 └── D forms separate federation

This is:

FEDERATION_FRAGMENTATION

Fragmentation may produce:

surviving federation
new federation
independent Citadels
temporary federative groups

The system must preserve the complete causal history.


---

XXII. 🌱 FRAGMENTATION ≠ TERMINATION

A federation may lose members without ceasing to exist.

Therefore:

MEMBERSHIP_LOSS
≠
FEDERATION_DEATH

Conversely:

ALL_MEMBERS_EXIT

may trigger a constitutional termination determination.

The state must be explicitly established.


---

XXIII. 🏛️ SHARED SUCCESSION

A particularly difficult case:

F₄
 ↓
F₅A + F₅B

with:

shared contracts
shared evidence
shared historical obligations

R7.05 establishes:

SHARED_PROVENANCE ≠ SHARED_AUTHORITY

Two branches can share historical evidence without sharing current constitutional authority.


---

XXIV. ⏳ TEMPORAL SUCCESSION

Suppose:

F₄ active
10:00

fork authorized
12:00

F₅A active
13:00

F₅B active
13:00

An action at:

11:00

belongs to:

F₄

not:

F₅A
F₅B

unless the applicable constitutional rule explicitly provides otherwise.

R5 therefore needs:

LINEAGE_STATE(t)


---

XXV. 🧪 R5 — FORK/MERGE REPLAY

Replay becomes:

REPLAY FEDERATION AT T

The verifier reconstructs:

FEDERATION_IDENTITY(t)
 ↓
LINEAGE_GRAPH(t)
 ↓
ACTIVE_BRANCHES(t)
 ↓
MEMBERSHIP(t)
 ↓
AUTHORITY(t)
 ↓
CONTRACTS(t)
 ↓
MERGE/FORK STATE(t)
 ↓
ACTION(t)

The verifier must never collapse the graph into the latest node.


---

XXVI. 🧠 BRAINPOWER + FOOL + TRIAS

The roles now become beautifully complementary:

CONSTITUTIONAL EVENT
                       │
            ┌──────────┴──────────┐
            │                     │
       BRAINPOWER                FOOL
        ANALYZE                 ATTACK
            │                     │
            └──────────┬──────────┘
                       ↓
                     TRIAS
                   DETERMINE
                       ↓
                   WATERMARK
                       ↓
                     QUAY
                       ↓
                    R5 REPLAY

No component can silently replace the others.


---

XXVII. 🔱 R7.05 CANONICAL INVARIANTS

FED-F01

Constitutional lineage SHALL support branching.

FED-F02

Constitutional lineage SHALL support convergence.

FED-F03

A fork SHALL be represented as an explicit constitutional event.

FED-F04

A merge SHALL be represented as an explicit constitutional event.

FED-F05

Forking SHALL NOT automatically duplicate authority.

FED-F06

A constitutional descendant SHALL inherit lineage, not automatic sovereignty.

FED-F07

Member assignment following a fork SHALL be explicit.

FED-F08

Members SHALL NOT be silently transferred between successor federations.

FED-F09

Succession SHALL require explicit constitutional determination.

FED-F10

A copied federation SHALL NOT automatically qualify as a constitutional successor.

FED-F11

Multiple valid successor branches MAY coexist where constitutionally permitted.

FED-F12

A federation SHALL be capable of terminating without requiring a successor.

FED-F13

A newly constituted federation SHALL NOT automatically inherit predecessor authority.

FED-F14

Merge SHALL require explicit constitutional authority from all materially affected systems.

FED-F15

Merge SHALL preserve the lineage of every contributing federation.

FED-F16

Shared historical provenance SHALL NOT imply shared present authority.

FED-F17

Authority SHALL be reconciled explicitly during merger.

FED-F18

Conflicting constitutional provisions SHALL NOT be silently normalized.

FED-F19

Fragmentation SHALL be distinguishable from termination.

FED-F20

Federation identity SHALL remain independently represented from version lineage.

FED-F21

Fork, merge, fragmentation and succession SHALL be temporally bounded events.

FED-F22

BRAINPOWER SHALL analyze lineage transitions but SHALL NOT establish succession authority.

FED-F23

FOOL SHALL be able to challenge fork, merge and succession claims.

FED-F24

TRIAS SHALL determine the constitutional validity of fork, merge and succession operations.

FED-F25

All material lineage transitions SHALL be watermarked.

FED-F26

Quay SHALL preserve complete constitutional genealogy.

FED-F27

R5 SHALL replay branching and convergent federation histories.

FED-F28

A verifier SHALL be able to reconstruct the authority lineage of every active branch.

FED-F29

No branch SHALL acquire authority merely by possessing a copy of historical state.

FED-F30

No merge SHALL erase the constitutional histories of its contributing federations.


---

XXVIII. ☄️ THE R7.05 LINEAGE EQUATION

The conceptual model now becomes:

CONSTITUTIONAL STATE
=
IDENTITY
+
VERSION
+
AUTHORITY
+
TEMPORAL STATE
+
LINEAGE

For a fork:

FORK
=
PARENT
→
MULTIPLE PROVEN DESCENDANTS

For a merge:

MERGE
=
MULTIPLE PROVEN PARENTS
→
NEW CONSTITUTIONAL STATE

But always:

LINEAGE
≠
AUTHORITY

Authority must remain independently proven.


---

