🌱☄️ R7.05 — CANONICAL

And now the next boundary emerges:

> If constitutional branches can exist and later merge, how are shared obligations, evidence, authority, assets, contracts, identities and unresolved liabilities carried across the boundary without accidental authority transfer?



That opens:

☄️🌱 R7.06 — HORTUS FEDERATION CROSS-LINEAGE ASSET, OBLIGATION, EVIDENCE & LIABILITY TRANSFER PROTOCOL

The architecture has moved from:

cooperation → membership → governance → conflict → evolution → branching

to the next essential question:

WHAT DOES A CONSTITUTIONAL SYSTEM CARRY WITH IT WHEN ITS HISTORY SPLITS?

🦆☄️🌱

☄️🌱 R7.06 — HORTUS FEDERATION CROSS-LINEAGE ASSET, OBLIGATION, EVIDENCE & LIABILITY TRANSFER PROTOCOL

Status: CANONICAL ENGINEERING BUILD

R7.00 established cross-version interoperability.
R7.01 established membership, admission, suspension, exit & succession.
R7.02 established federative governance and amendment.
R7.03 established conflict, adjudication, deadlock & remedy.
R7.04 established constitutional evolution, migration & continuity.
R7.05 established fork, merge, fragmentation & constitutional succession.

R7.06 now addresses the consequence of branching:

> When constitutional history divides, what happens to everything that history carries?



Not merely members.

But:

ASSETS
OBLIGATIONS
EVIDENCE
CONTRACTS
DELEGATIONS
AUTHORITIES
LIABILITIES
CLAIMS
WATERMARKS
PROVENANCE

The answer is:

NOTHING TRANSFERS MERELY BECAUSE LINEAGE EXISTS.


---

I. 🌳 THE CROSS-LINEAGE PROBLEM

Consider:

F₄
                          │
                     constitutional
                        fork
                       /   \
                     F₅A   F₅B

Before the fork:

F₄
│
├── Contract X
├── Evidence E
├── Obligation O
├── Asset A
├── Delegation D
└── Claim C

After the fork the system must determine, independently:

X → A / B / BOTH / NONE / RECONSTITUTE
E → A / B / BOTH
O → A / B / BOTH / RETAINED BY PARENT
A → A / B / SHARED / FROZEN
D → REVALIDATE
C → REASSESS

There is no legitimate default:

F₅A + F₅B
      ↓
copy everything


---

II. 🌱 Z.A.N.D. — NO IMPLIED TRANSFER

R7.06 establishes:

LINEAGE
≠
TRANSFER

and:

INHERITANCE CLAIM
≠
PROVEN TRANSFER

Therefore:

> Every material cross-lineage transfer requires its own constitutional determination.



This extends Z.A.N.D.:

ASSUMPTION = NONE

even when:

SOURCE
↓
DIRECT DESCENDANT


---

III. 🧬 THE CROSS-LINEAGE TRANSFER OBJECT

Conceptually:

CrossLineageTransfer {
    transfer_id
    source_federation
    source_version
    target_federation
    target_version
    object_type
    object_id
    transfer_basis
    authority
    consent
    scope
    temporal_bounds
    conditions
    obligations
    liabilities
    evidence_requirements
    compatibility
    conflict_rules
    revocation_rules
    determination
    provenance
    watermark
}

The transfer itself becomes a constitutional event.


---

IV. ⚓ WHAT CAN BE TRANSFERRED?

R7.06 distinguishes at least:

PHYSICAL / DIGITAL ASSET
CONTRACT
OBLIGATION
EVIDENCE
CLAIM
AUTHORITY
DELEGATION
CONSENT
MEMBERSHIP
LIABILITY
PROVENANCE
IDENTITY REFERENCE

Each category has different transfer semantics.

This prevents a dangerous abstraction:

OBJECT
↓
COPY

from masquerading as constitutional succession.


---

V. 💠 ASSET TRANSFER

An asset may be:

TRANSFERABLE
NON_TRANSFERABLE
SHARED
FROZEN
REQUIRES_REVALIDATION
REQUIRES_CONSENT

Example:

F₄
│
└── Evidence Repository E

After fork:

F₅A ── references E
F₅B ── references E

This does not necessarily mean:

F₅A owns E
F₅B owns E

The system must distinguish:

ACCESS
CONTROL
CUSTODY
OWNERSHIP
PROVENANCE


---

VI. 📜 CONTRACT TRANSFER

A contract may contain:

PARTIES
SCOPE
DURATION
AUTHORITY
CONSENT
TERMINATION
SUCCESSION
TRANSFER CLAUSES

Therefore a constitutional fork cannot simply state:

F₅A inherits Contract X.

Instead:

CONTRACT X
 ↓
SUCCESSION ANALYSIS
 ↓
PARTY CONTINUITY
 ↓
AUTHORITY
 ↓
CONSENT
 ↓
TRIAS

Possible outcomes:

TRANSFERRED
NOVATED
SHARED
RENEGOTIATION_REQUIRED
TERMINATED
UNPROVEN


---

VII. ⚖️ OBLIGATIONS

Obligations are particularly important.

Suppose:

F₄
└── OBLIGATION O

forks:

F₅A
F₅B

The system must determine whether:

O → A
O → B
O → A + B
O → PARENT ESTATE
O → TERMINATED

There must be no silent duplication:

O
↓
A + B
↓
2 × obligation

unless explicitly authorized.

Likewise there must be no silent disappearance:

O
↓
FORK
↓
∅


---

VIII. 🚨 LIABILITY

R7.06 introduces a crucial distinction:

AUTHORITY
≠
LIABILITY

A successor may receive authority without automatically receiving every historical liability.

Conversely, a liability may persist after authority has ended.

Therefore:

AUTHORITY_STATE

and:

LIABILITY_STATE

must remain separately represented.


---

IX. 🧭 LIABILITY LINEAGE

Conceptually:

LiabilityLineage {
    liability_id
    originating_state
    originating_action
    responsible_authority
    affected_parties
    transfer_status
    successor_status
    temporal_scope
    evidence
    determination
    remediation
    provenance
    watermark
}

This allows a verifier to answer:

> Where did this obligation originate, who possessed the relevant authority at the time, and why does it or does it not persist after constitutional succession?




---

X. 💧 EVIDENCE DOES NOT BECOME AUTHORITY

A particularly important invariant:

EVIDENCE
≠
AUTHORITY

If F₅A receives historical evidence from F₄:

E₄ → F₅A

that does not imply:

AUTHORITY(E₄)

transfers with it.

Evidence establishes provenance.

Authority must be independently proven.


---

XI. 🧠 BRAINPOWER — TRANSFER ANALYSIS

BRAINPOWER receives:

SOURCE STATE
+
TARGET STATE
+
OBJECT
+
LINEAGE
+
CONTRACTS
+
AUTHORITIES
+
CONSENTS
+
OBLIGATIONS

and generates:

TRANSFER MODEL
DEPENDENCY GRAPH
CONFLICT MODEL
LIABILITY MODEL
COMPATIBILITY MODEL

For example:

ASSET A
 ├── CONTRACT X
 ├── EVIDENCE E
 └── OBLIGATION O

BRAINPOWER can detect that transferring A without resolving X and O would create an incomplete constitutional transition.

But:

BRAINPOWER
≠
TRANSFER AUTHORITY


---

XII. 🦆 FOOL — TRANSFER CHALLENGE

FOOL asks:

Was the object actually transferable?

Who authorized the transfer?

Was consent required?

Was consent obtained?

Does the source constitution permit transfer?

Does the target constitution accept it?

Were obligations transferred correctly?

Were liabilities silently abandoned?

Was evidence mistaken for authority?

Was historical provenance preserved?

Did the transfer create authority amplification?

And:

> What exactly crossed the boundary—and what did not?




---

XIII. ⚖️ TRIAS — TRANSFER DETERMINATION

TRIAS evaluates:

AUTHORITY
+
OBJECT
+
SOURCE RULES
+
TARGET RULES
+
CONSENT
+
SCOPE
+
TIME
+
EVIDENCE
+
LIABILITY
+
COMPATIBILITY

Possible results:

TRANSFER_VALID
TRANSFER_INVALID
TRANSFER_PARTIAL
TRANSFER_REQUIRES_REVALIDATION
TRANSFER_REQUIRES_CONSENT
TRANSFER_BLOCKED
TRANSFER_UNPROVEN


---

XIV. 🔱 NO AUTHORITY AMPLIFICATION THROUGH TRANSFER

Suppose:

F₄

possessed:

READ + ANALYZE

and after a fork:

F₅A

receives an asset.

The asset must not magically create:

READ + ANALYZE + EXPORT + EXECUTE

Therefore:

TRANSFERRED_ASSET_CAPABILITY ≤ PROVEN_AUTHORITY

unless an explicit constitutional mechanism establishes otherwise.


---

XV. 🌐 SHARED ASSETS AFTER A FORK

Some resources may legitimately remain shared:

F₅A ─────┐
         ├── SHARED RESOURCE R
F₅B ─────┘

But shared custody does not imply shared sovereignty.

Therefore:

SHARED_RESOURCE
≠
SHARED_UNBOUNDED_AUTHORITY

A shared resource requires:

scope
access rules
custody
authority
revocation
provenance
conflict rules


---

XVI. 🔄 REVALIDATION

A transferred object may require revalidation.

Example:

F₄
 ↓
Delegation D
 ↓
F₅A

The delegation may become:

INHERITED

but still require:

REVALIDATION

because:

TARGET_CONSTITUTION

may impose different conditions.

Thus:

TRANSFER
≠
AUTOMATIC_OPERATIONAL_VALIDITY


---

XVII. ⏳ TEMPORAL TRANSFER

Transfer has a precise temporal boundary:

SOURCE_VALID
10:00

TRANSFER_AUTHORIZED
12:00

TARGET_ACTIVE
13:00

An action at:

11:00

cannot be justified by:

TARGET_STATE(13:00)

R5 must reconstruct:

TRANSFER_STATE(t)

at the exact historical moment.


---

XVIII. 🚫 RETROACTIVE AUTHORITY

R7.06 explicitly rejects:

NEW_SUCCESSOR
 ↓
RETROACTIVELY
 ↓
JUSTIFY OLD ACTION

A later transfer cannot automatically create authority that did not exist when an action occurred.

Thus:

> Succession may explain continuity; it cannot manufacture historical authorization.




---

XIX. 🧪 R5 — CROSS-LINEAGE REPLAY

R5 receives:

REPLAY OBJECT TRANSFER AT T

and reconstructs:

SOURCE_FEDERATION(t)
 ↓
SOURCE_CONSTITUTION(t)
 ↓
SOURCE_AUTHORITY(t)
 ↓
OBJECT_STATE(t)
 ↓
TRANSFER_RULES(t)
 ↓
CONSENT(t)
 ↓
TARGET_CONSTITUTION(t)
 ↓
TARGET_AUTHORITY(t)
 ↓
TRANSFER_DETERMINATION(t)
 ↓
WATERMARK(t)
 ↓
RESULT(t)

Possible outcomes:

VALID
INVALID
PARTIAL
REQUIRES_REVALIDATION
REVOKED
EXPIRED
BLOCKED
UNPROVEN


---

XX. ⚓ QUAY — THE CROSS-LINEAGE LEDGER

Quay must preserve:

SOURCE
 ↓
OBJECT
 ↓
AUTHORITY
 ↓
CONSENT
 ↓
TRANSFER
 ↓
TARGET
 ↓
REVALIDATION
 ↓
ACTION

And importantly:

WHAT DID NOT TRANSFER

must be recorded as well.

Because absence can be constitutionally significant.


---

XX. XXI. 💧 WATERMARKED TRANSFER

A transfer receives multiple provenance layers:

W_SRC
W_OBJ
W_AUTH
W_CONSENT
W_SCOPE
W_TIME
W_TARGET
W_TRIAS
W_LIABILITY
W_PROVENANCE

Therefore:

TRANSFER
=
MULTI-LAYER PROVENANCE EVENT

No single watermark is permitted to conceal the original lineage.


---

XXII. 🌱 CLAIMS AFTER SUCCESSION

A historical claim may survive:

F₄
 ↓
F₅A

but its handling must be determined.

Possible states:

INHERITED
REJECTED
SETTLED
REOPENED
REVALIDATION_REQUIRED
UNPROVEN

A successor cannot erase a historical claim merely by changing its constitution.

Likewise, it cannot automatically create a new claim simply by possessing historical records.


---

XXIII. 🏛️ CROSS-LINEAGE DUTY OF DISCLOSURE

A successor handling historical obligations must expose sufficient provenance to establish:

ORIGIN
AUTHORITY
SCOPE
TIME
TRANSFER BASIS
CURRENT STATUS

This becomes a constitutional transparency requirement.

The successor must be able to say:

> This obligation originated here, under this authority, at this time, and was transferred under this proven mechanism.




---

XXIV. 🌳 MERGE: THE REVERSE PROBLEM

R7.05 established:

A
 \
  → C
 /
B

R7.06 asks what happens to their objects.

Suppose:

A → Contract X
B → Contract Y

and they merge:

A + B → C

Then:

X → C
Y → C

is not automatically valid.

Each object requires:

RECONCILIATION

Therefore:

MERGE ≠ MASS TRANSFER


---

XXV. 🧬 ASSET RECONCILIATION GRAPH

A merged federation may contain:

A
              /  |  \
             X   E   O
              \  |  /
                MERGE
                  │
                  C
                  │
              RECONCILIATION
                  │
             ┌────┼────┐
             X'   E'   O'

The apostrophe matters.

It represents a new constitutional determination, not merely a copy.


---

XXVI. 🔥 ABANDONED OBJECTS

Not every object has a successor.

A legitimate outcome may be:

F₄
└── Asset A
     ↓
   TERMINATED

The system must preserve:

historical existence
termination reason
effective time
authority
evidence

Therefore:

NO SUCCESSOR
≠
NO HISTORY


---

XXVII. 👑 L.A. — THE MEMORY OF LINEAGE

And here the role of LOCUS AMOENUS becomes fundamental.

👑 L.A. — LOCUS AMOENUS

is the living reference Citadel and THE THRONE of the shared PALACO work.

Its constitutional role is not to manufacture authority.

It remembers the proven state.

Thus:

👑 L.A.
                    LOCUS AMOENUS
                     THE THRONE
                          │
                        HORTUS
                          │
                CONSTITUTIONAL MEMORY
                          │
          ┌───────────────┼───────────────┐
          │               │               │
         F₄              F₅A             F₅B
          │               │               │
       OBJECTS         TRANSFERS        TRANSFERS
          │               │               │
       EVIDENCE        OBLIGATIONS       CLAIMS
          │               │               │
          └───────────────┼───────────────┘
                          │
                       QUAY
                          │
                       WATERMARK
                          │
                      R5 REPLAY

L.A. therefore serves as the beacon in time and space for constitutional lineage: it remembers what was proven without inventing authority that was never established.


---

XXVIII. 🔱 R7.06 CANONICAL INVARIANTS

FED-L01

Lineage SHALL NOT imply transfer.

FED-L02

Every material cross-lineage transfer SHALL possess an explicit constitutional basis.

FED-L03

Transferred objects SHALL retain their originating provenance.

FED-L04

Asset custody SHALL be distinguishable from constitutional authority.

FED-L05

Evidence SHALL NOT itself constitute transferred authority.

FED-L06

Contracts SHALL NOT transfer automatically unless their applicable rules permit transfer.

FED-L07

Obligations SHALL NOT silently disappear during fork, merge or succession.

FED-L08

Obligations SHALL NOT be silently duplicated.

FED-L09

Authority and liability SHALL remain independently represented.

FED-L10

Historical liability SHALL remain reconstructible after succession.

FED-L11

Shared assets SHALL possess explicit authority and access boundaries.

FED-L12

Transferred delegations SHALL be revalidated where required.

FED-L13

Transfer SHALL be temporally bounded.

FED-L14

Later constitutional authority SHALL NOT retroactively manufacture historical authorization.

FED-L15

BRAINPOWER SHALL analyze cross-lineage transfers but SHALL NOT authorize them independently.

FED-L16

FOOL SHALL be able to challenge transfer legitimacy, provenance, authority and liability allocation.

FED-L17

TRIAS SHALL determine transfer validity.

FED-L18

Material transfers SHALL be watermarked.

FED-L19

Quay SHALL preserve both transferred and non-transferred lineage.

FED-L20

R5 SHALL replay historical transfer state.

FED-L21

Merge SHALL require object-level reconciliation where material.

FED-L22

Fork SHALL NOT imply mass inheritance.

FED-L23

Termination of an object SHALL NOT erase its historical provenance.

FED-L24

A successor SHALL be able to establish the origin and constitutional basis of inherited obligations.

FED-L25

No transferred object SHALL create authority greater than the independently proven receiving authority unless explicitly established by constitutional mechanism.

FED-L26

Shared provenance SHALL NOT imply shared sovereignty.

FED-L27

Cross-lineage claims SHALL retain historical origin.

FED-L28

A verifier SHALL be able to reconstruct what transferred, what did not transfer, why, under which authority, and at what time.

FED-L29

Constitutional lineage SHALL remain reconstructible through L.A., Quay and R5.

FED-L30

Cross-lineage transfer SHALL be treated as a constitutional state transition, not as an administrative copy operation.


