XII. 🌐 DISTRIBUTED TRUST

Where appropriate:

ANCHOR A
ANCHOR B
ANCHOR C

may jointly support:

FEDERATED SERVICE

But this does not mean:

3 anchors
=
majority determines truth

Instead, the protocol must define exactly what threshold means.

For example:

2-of-3

may establish:

SERVICE_INTEGRITY

without establishing:

CONSTITUTIONAL_TRUTH

This distinction is essential.


---

XIII. ⚖️ CONSENSUS ≠ TRUTH — AGAIN

R7.07 reinforces the earlier principle:

CONSENSUS
≠
TRUTH

A shared infrastructure service may use consensus for:

availability
ordering
replication
fault tolerance

But consensus does not automatically establish:

constitutional validity

That remains subject to the constitutional determination chain.


---

XIV. 🔒 COMMON SERVICE BOUNDARY

Every shared service receives:

SERVICE_SCOPE

Conceptually:

SERVICE
├── permitted_operations
├── prohibited_operations
├── participants
├── resources
├── authority
├── temporal_bounds
└── failure_behavior

Anything outside that scope is:

OUT_OF_SCOPE


---

XV. 🚫 NO SERVICE AUTHORITY ESCALATION

Suppose a service is authorized to:

VERIFY_EVIDENCE

It must not silently gain:

MODIFY_EVIDENCE

or:

REVOKE_AUTHORITY

or:

ADMIT_CITADEL

or:

CHANGE_CONSTITUTION

Thus:

SERVICE_CAPABILITY ≤ PROVEN_SERVICE_AUTHORITY


---

XVI. 🔄 REVOCATION

If a shared service becomes untrusted:

TRUST ANCHOR REVOKED

then dependent operations must be reevaluated.

REVOCATION
 ↓
DEPENDENCY GRAPH
 ↓
AFFECTED SERVICES
 ↓
AFFECTED ACTIONS
 ↓
RE-EVALUATION

No stale trust may survive merely because cached state says:

VALID


---

XVII. ⏳ TEMPORAL SERVICE VALIDITY

A service may be valid:

10:00 → 14:00

and invalid:

14:01

Historical replay must therefore evaluate:

SERVICE_STATE(t)

not:

SERVICE_STATE(now)

This applies to:

keys
registries
time authorities
evidence anchors
operators
contracts
trust relationships


---

XVIII. 🧪 R5 — INFRASTRUCTURE REPLAY

R5 now reconstructs:

REPLAY SHARED SERVICE AT T

with:

SERVICE_IDENTITY(t)
 ↓
OPERATOR(t)
 ↓
AUTHORITY(t)
 ↓
TRUST_ANCHORS(t)
 ↓
PARTICIPANTS(t)
 ↓
SERVICE_SCOPE(t)
 ↓
DEPENDENCIES(t)
 ↓
REVOCATIONS(t)
 ↓
FAILURES(t)
 ↓
ACTION(t)

The verifier can then establish:

SERVICE_VALID
SERVICE_INVALID
SERVICE_UNTRUSTED
SERVICE_OUT_OF_SCOPE
SERVICE_EXPIRED
SERVICE_REVOKED
SERVICE_UNPROVEN


---

XIX. ⚓ QUAY — INFRASTRUCTURE PROVENANCE

Quay preserves:

WHO
WHAT
WHEN
WHY
UNDER WHICH AUTHORITY
USING WHICH SERVICE
UNDER WHICH TRUST ANCHOR
FOR WHICH CITADELS
WITH WHICH SCOPE

The resulting provenance chain:

CITADEL
 ↓
CONSTITUTION
 ↓
AUTHORITY
 ↓
FEDERATION
 ↓
SERVICE CONTRACT
 ↓
SERVICE
 ↓
TRUST ANCHOR
 ↓
OPERATION
 ↓
EVIDENCE
 ↓
WATERMARK
 ↓
QUAY


---

XX. 🌱 SERVICE FAILURE

A constitutional infrastructure service can fail.

R7.07 therefore distinguishes:

SERVICE_FAILURE
SERVICE_COMPROMISE
SERVICE_UNAVAILABLE
SERVICE_UNTRUSTED
SERVICE_REVOKED
SERVICE_OBSOLETE

These are not equivalent.

A failed service may still have historically valid evidence.

A compromised service may invalidate new evidence.

An unavailable service may require a fallback.

An obsolete service may remain valid for historical replay.


---

XXI. 🧭 FAIL-SAFE PRINCIPLE

When infrastructure is unavailable, PALACO must not automatically assume:

FAILURE
↓
ALLOW

Nor:

FAILURE
↓
DENY EVERYTHING FOREVER

Instead:

FAILURE
 ↓
FAILURE POLICY
 ↓
CONSTITUTIONAL DETERMINATION

Possible outcomes:

BLOCK
DEFER
FALLBACK
LOCAL_ONLY
EMERGENCY_MODE
REVALIDATE


---

XXII. 🚨 EMERGENCY INFRASTRUCTURE

If an emergency mechanism exists:

EMERGENCY_SERVICE

it must possess:

EXPLICIT SCOPE
EXPLICIT TRIGGER
EXPLICIT AUTHORITY
EXPLICIT DURATION
EXPLICIT EXIT
EXPLICIT AUDIT

Emergency infrastructure must never become:

PERMANENT_EXCEPTION


---

XXIII. 🏛️ SERVICE GOVERNANCE

Shared infrastructure itself requires governance.

But this creates a recursion problem:

WHO GOVERNS THE GOVERNANCE SERVICE?

R7.07 resolves this by separation:

SERVICE GOVERNANCE
≠
SERVICE OPERATION
≠
CONSTITUTIONAL AUTHORITY

Different roles may be assigned to:

operators
auditors
maintainers
constitutional authorities
independent verifiers

with independently bounded scopes.


---

XXIV. 🌳 INFRASTRUCTURE DEPENDENCY GRAPH

HORTUS now acquires another graph:

FEDERATION
                     │
          ┌──────────┼──────────┐
          │          │          │
        QUAY       TIME       IDENTITY
          │          │          │
       EVIDENCE    CLOCK      REGISTRY
          │
      WATERMARK
          │
        REPLAY

Every dependency becomes visible.

This prevents hidden infrastructure authority.


---

XXV. 💠 SERVICE EXIT

What happens when a Citadel leaves?

Suppose:

A ─┐
B ─┼── SHARED SERVICE
C ─┘

and:

B EXIT

The service must determine:

CAN_CONTINUE
REQUIRES_RECONFIGURATION
REQUIRES_RECONSENT
MUST_TERMINATE

B's historical participation remains preserved.

Its exit does not erase:

B'S PAST SERVICE EVENTS


---

XXVI. 🧬 SERVICE SUCCESSION

If an operator changes:

OPERATOR A
     ↓
OPERATOR B

then:

SERVICE_IDENTITY

may continue while:

OPERATOR_AUTHORITY

changes.

The transfer requires:

SUCCESSION AUTHORITY
CONSENT
EVIDENCE
SCOPE
TEMPORAL BOUNDARY

Again:

SERVICE CONTINUITY
≠
OPERATOR CONTINUITY


---

XXVII. 🔱 R7.07 CANONICAL INVARIANTS

FED-S01

Shared infrastructure SHALL NOT acquire implicit constitutional sovereignty.

FED-S02

Service capability SHALL remain distinct from constitutional authority.

FED-S03

Every shared service SHALL possess explicit scope.

FED-S04

Every shared service operator SHALL possess explicitly bounded authority.

FED-S05

Trust anchors SHALL be purpose-bound and scope-bound.

FED-S06

Trust SHALL NOT be inferred beyond its declared scope.

FED-S07

A trust anchor SHALL NOT automatically become a constitutional authority.

FED-S08

Shared services SHALL preserve participant sovereignty.

FED-S09

Shared infrastructure SHALL NOT silently modify constitutional state.

FED-S10

Shared infrastructure SHALL NOT silently amplify authority.

FED-S11

Material service operations SHALL be evidenced.

FED-S12

Material service operations SHALL be watermarkable.

FED-S13

Service dependencies SHALL be explicitly represented.

FED-S14

Critical infrastructure SHALL expose constitutional failure behavior.

FED-S15

Service failure SHALL NOT automatically imply authorization.

FED-S16

Emergency infrastructure SHALL be explicitly bounded.

FED-S17

Emergency infrastructure SHALL possess an explicit termination condition.

FED-S18

Service revocation SHALL propagate to affected dependent evaluations.

FED-S19

Historical service validity SHALL be determined using the applicable state at time t.

FED-S20

Service succession SHALL preserve operator and authority provenance.

FED-S21

Service exit SHALL NOT erase historical participation.

FED-S22

Shared infrastructure governance SHALL remain distinct from infrastructure operation.

FED-S23

BRAINPOWER SHALL analyze infrastructure trust and dependency models but SHALL NOT independently authorize them.

FED-S24

FOOL SHALL be able to challenge service authority, dependency, trust and governance boundaries.

FED-S25

TRIAS SHALL determine constitutional validity of shared infrastructure arrangements.

FED-S26

Quay SHALL preserve infrastructure provenance.

FED-S27

R5 SHALL replay shared infrastructure state historically.

FED-S28

Infrastructure SHALL NOT become an implicit constitutional gatekeeper merely through dependency.

FED-S29

Consensus mechanisms used by infrastructure SHALL NOT automatically determine constitutional truth.

FED-S30

A verifier SHALL be able to reconstruct which infrastructure supported an action and why that infrastructure was constitutionally valid at the relevant time.


