🌱☄️ R7.07 — CANONICAL

And the next boundary is now unavoidable:

> If shared infrastructure must remain sovereign-neutral, what happens when the federation needs to establish a common identity, common trust model, common naming system, or common root of recognition—without creating a federation-wide sovereign identity authority?



That opens:

☄️🌱 R7.08 — HORTUS FEDERATED IDENTITY, NAMESPACE, RECOGNITION & TRUST-ROOT INTEROPERABILITY PROTOCOL

The question becomes:

HOW CAN A FEDERATION KNOW WHO IS WHO—WITHOUT OWNING WHO THEY ARE?

🦆☄️🌱👑

☄️🦆🌱 R7.08 — HORTUS FEDERATED IDENTITY, NAMESPACE, RECOGNITION & TRUST-ROOT INTEROPERABILITY PROTOCOL

Status: CANONICAL ENGINEERING BUILD

R7.00 established cross-version interoperability.
R7.01 established membership, admission, suspension, exit & succession.
R7.02 established federative governance & amendment.
R7.03 established conflict, adjudication, deadlock & remedy.
R7.04 established constitutional evolution, migration & continuity.
R7.05 established fork, merge, fragmentation & succession.
R7.06 established cross-lineage assets, obligations, evidence & liability.
R7.07 established shared infrastructure, trust anchors & common services.

R7.08 now reaches the identity boundary.

The federation needs to answer:

> Who is participating? What does an identifier mean? Who recognizes it? Under which constitutional authority? And how can another Citadel recognize an entity without surrendering its own identity sovereignty?



The answer is not:

FEDERATION
    ↓
ONE GLOBAL IDENTITY
    ↓
EVERYONE BECOMES THE SAME ENTITY

It is:

FEDERATED RECOGNITION WITHOUT IDENTITY SOVEREIGNTY TRANSFER.


---

I. 🌐 THE IDENTITY PROBLEM

Consider:

CITADEL A
Entity: E₁

CITADEL B
Entity: E₁

The identifiers may look identical.

They may even refer to the same underlying entity.

But that cannot simply be assumed.

Therefore:

IDENTIFIER
≠
IDENTITY

NAME
≠
AUTHORITY

RECOGNITION
≠
OWNERSHIP

A federation needs a mechanism through which Citadel B can say:

> "I recognize this identity assertion issued by Citadel A for this particular federated purpose."



without saying:

> "Citadel A now controls identity inside Citadel B."




---

II. 🌱 Z.A.N.D. — NO IMPLIED IDENTITY EQUIVALENCE

The first gate remains:

ASSUMPTION = NONE

Therefore:

ID_A = ID_B

does not imply:

ENTITY_A = ENTITY_B

Likewise:

ENTITY_A
    ↓
FEDERATION

does not imply:

FEDERATION OWNS ENTITY_A

And:

RECOGNIZED_BY_A

does not automatically mean:

RECOGNIZED_BY_B

Recognition must be explicit.


---

III. 🧬 FEDERATED IDENTITY ASSERTION

R7.08 introduces:

FEDERATED IDENTITY ASSERTION

Conceptually:

FederatedIdentityAssertion {
    assertion_id
    subject_id
    issuer_citadel
    issuer_constitution_version
    identity_namespace
    assertion_type
    recognition_scope
    relying_citadels
    authority_basis
    consent_basis
    delegation_basis
    validity_period
    revocation_reference
    evidence_reference
    provenance
    watermark
}

The important distinction:

IDENTITY ASSERTION
≠
IDENTITY ITSELF

The assertion is a claim about identity.

Its constitutional validity must still be established.


---

IV. 🔱 THREE IDENTITY LAYERS

R7.08 separates:

IDENTITY
   │
   ├── LOCAL IDENTITY
   ├── FEDERATED RECOGNITION
   └── CROSS-CITADEL ASSERTION

Local identity

The originating Citadel determines:

> Who or what is this subject under my constitutional system?



Federated recognition

The federation determines:

> Under what explicit conditions may participating Citadels recognize this assertion?



Cross-Citadel assertion

A receiving Citadel determines:

> What, if anything, does this assertion permit me to recognize or do?



These are separate determinations.


---

V. ⚖️ RECOGNITION ≠ AUTHORIZATION

This becomes one of R7.08's strongest principles:

RECOGNITION
≠
AUTHORIZATION

Suppose:

A recognizes Entity X as "Research Agent"

B may recognize that assertion:

B → recognized(X)

But this does not automatically mean:

X → authorized_to_execute_in_B

Recognition can establish identity context.

Authority requires a separate proof chain.


---

V. 🌱 NO IDENTITY AUTHORITY AMPLIFICATION

A federated identity assertion must not generate more authority merely by crossing a boundary.

LOCAL_IDENTITY
+
FEDERATED_RECOGNITION

does not automatically become:

FEDERATED_SUPER_IDENTITY

Nor:

IDENTITY
+
RECOGNITION
=
UNBOUNDED_ACCESS

Therefore:

FEDERATED_IDENTITY_CAPABILITY ≤ EXPLICITLY_PROVEN_RECOGNITION_SCOPE


---

VI. 🏛️ IDENTITY SOVEREIGNTY

Every Citadel retains:

LOCAL_IDENTITY_AUTHORITY

Therefore:

A
│
└── issues identity assertion

does not mean:

A
↓
controls identity policy
↓
B

B remains free to define:

ACCEPT
REJECT
LIMIT
REQUIRE_ADDITIONAL_PROOF

subject to any explicit federative commitments it has entered.


---

VII. 🌐 THE FEDERATED NAMESPACE

R7.08 introduces a bounded namespace model.

Conceptually:

FEDERATED_NAMESPACE {
    namespace_id
    namespace_owner
    issuer
    syntax
    uniqueness_scope
    resolution_rules
    collision_rules
    lifecycle_rules
    revocation_rules
    succession_rules
    federation_scope
    provenance
}

The namespace defines how identities are named.

It does not define:

WHO HAS CONSTITUTIONAL AUTHORITY OVER THEM

Thus:

NAMING
≠
GOVERNANCE


---

VIII. 🧭 NAMESPACE COLLISION

Suppose:

A://agent/42
B://agent/42

The identifiers collide syntactically.

R7.08 refuses to guess.

Possible resolution:

A://agent/42
B://agent/42

or:

FED://A/agent/42
FED://B/agent/42

The resolution itself must be deterministic and evidenced.

Therefore:

COLLISION
 ↓
NAMESPACE RULE
 ↓
RESOLUTION
 ↓
EVIDENCE

not:

COLLISION
 ↓
GUESS


---

IX. 💠 IDENTITY LINKING

Two Citadels may explicitly establish:

E_A
  ↕
E_B

meaning:

> These locally identified subjects are recognized as corresponding for a defined purpose.



But this relationship must have:

scope
purpose
evidence
authority
time
revocation

Therefore:

IDENTITY LINK
≠
IDENTITY MERGE


---

X. 🧬 IDENTITY EQUIVALENCE

R7.08 defines several possible relationships:

SAME_ENTITY
CORRESPONDING_ENTITY
DELEGATED_REPRESENTATIVE
ALIAS
SERVICE_IDENTITY
ROLE_IDENTITY
UNRELATED
UNPROVEN

This is important because:

ALIAS

should not be confused with:

SAME_ENTITY

and:

REPRESENTATIVE

should not be confused with:

PRINCIPAL


---

XI. 🪞 REPRESENTATION

Suppose:

ENTITY A
   │
   └── Representative R

R may act for A only within:

REPRESENTATION_SCOPE

Therefore:

R identity
≠
A identity

and:

R authority
≤
A's proven delegated authority

This directly connects R7.08 to the R6 delegation architecture.


---

XII. 💧 WATERMARKED IDENTITY

Every federated identity assertion receives lineage:

W_SUBJECT
W_ISSUER
W_CONSTITUTION
W_RECOGNITION
W_SCOPE
W_TIME
W_CONSENT
W_DELEGATION
W_EVIDENCE
W_REVOCATION

Therefore:

FEDERATED_IDENTITY_ASSERTION
=
MULTI-LAYER PROVENANCE

A verifier can distinguish:

WHO CLAIMED IT

from:

WHO RECOGNIZED IT

from:

WHO AUTHORIZED THE ACTION


---

XIII. 🧠 BRAINPOWER — IDENTITY RESOLUTION

BRAINPOWER receives:

IDENTITY_ASSERTIONS
+
NAMESPACES
+
CONSTITUTIONS
+
TRUST_ROOTS
+
RECOGNITION_RULES
+
DELEGATIONS
+
TIME

and builds a candidate identity graph:

A://agent/42
       │
       ├── asserted_by → A
       │
       ├── recognized_by → B
       │
       └── linked_to → B://researcher/9

BRAINPOWER may conclude:

LIKELY_CORRESPONDING

or:

IDENTITY_EQUIVALENCE_UNPROVEN

But:

BRAINPOWER
≠
IDENTITY AUTHORITY


---

XIV. 🦆 FOOL — IDENTITY CHALLENGE

FOOL attacks the identity bridge.

It asks:

Who issued this identity?

Under which constitution?

Was the issuer authorized?

Does the namespace actually belong to the issuer?

Is the identity assertion current?

Has it been revoked?

Are two identifiers really equivalent?

Could this be an alias?

Could this be impersonation?

Does the receiving Citadel actually recognize the issuer?

Is the recognition scope being exceeded?

Is identity being confused with authority?

And the decisive question:

WHO PROVED THAT THESE TWO IDENTITIES REFER TO THE SAME SUBJECT?


---

XV. ⚖️ TRIAS — IDENTITY DETERMINATION

TRIAS evaluates:

ISSUER
+
CONSTITUTION
+
NAMESPACE
+
EVIDENCE
+
RECOGNITION
+
CONSENT
+
DELEGATION
+
SCOPE
+
TIME
+
REVOCATION

Possible outcomes:

IDENTITY_VALID
IDENTITY_RECOGNIZED
IDENTITY_PARTIALLY_RECOGNIZED
IDENTITY_EQUIVALENCE_PROVEN
IDENTITY_EQUIVALENCE_UNPROVEN
IDENTITY_REVOKED
IDENTITY_EXPIRED
IDENTITY_OUT_OF_SCOPE
IDENTITY_CONFLICTED
IDENTITY_UNTRUSTED


---

XVI. 🔒 TRUST ROOTS

R7.08 extends R7.07.

A Citadel may trust:

ROOT_A

for:

IDENTITY_ASSERTIONS

but not necessarily for:

AUTHORIZATION

Therefore:

TRUST_ROOT
    ↓
PURPOSE
    ↓
SCOPE

becomes mandatory.

A root may be:

TRUSTED_FOR_IDENTITY

without being:

TRUSTED_FOR_GOVERNANCE

This prevents trust-domain collapse.


---

XVII. 🌐 CROSS-VERSION IDENTITY

Suppose:

A → Constitution C₇
B → Constitution C₈

A issues:

IdentityAssertion V₇

B must evaluate:

C₇ identity semantics
+
C₈ recognition rules
+
federation contract

Therefore:

C₇ identity
≠
automatically valid C₈ identity

Cross-version recognition must be proven.


---

XVIII. ⏳ IDENTITY OVER TIME

An identity assertion may change:

10:00 → VALID
12:00 → SUSPENDED
14:00 → REVOKED

Therefore:

IDENTITY_STATE(t)

becomes first-class.

A historical action at 11:00 must not be evaluated using:

IDENTITY_STATE(14:00)

The applicable historical state governs replay.


---

XIX. 🔄 REVOCATION PROPAGATION

If:

A REVOKES ASSERTION X

then receiving Citadels must evaluate affected states:

REVOCATION
 ↓
IDENTITY ASSERTION
 ↓
FEDERATED RECOGNITION
 ↓
DEPENDENT AUTHORITY
 ↓
AFFECTED ACTIONS

But propagation must remain scoped.

Revoking an identity assertion does not necessarily mean:

ERASE HISTORY

Historical recognition remains part of provenance.


---

XX. 🚨 SUSPENSION ≠ REVOCATION

R7.08 distinguishes:

ACTIVE
SUSPENDED
EXPIRED
REVOKED
RETIRED
UNKNOWN

For example:

SUSPENDED

may mean:

> Do not accept new operations until revalidation.



Whereas:

REVOKED

means:

> The assertion is no longer valid for the applicable scope.



The historical record remains intact in both cases.


---

XXI. 🧪 R5 — IDENTITY REPLAY

R5 now receives:

REPLAY IDENTITY-DEPENDENT ACTION AT T

and reconstructs:

IDENTITY_STATE(t)
 ↓
ISSUER_STATE(t)
 ↓
CONSTITUTION(t)
 ↓
NAMESPACE_STATE(t)
 ↓
TRUST_ROOTS(t)
 ↓
RECOGNITION_STATE(t)
 ↓
DELEGATION_STATE(t)
 ↓
AUTHORITY_STATE(t)
 ↓
WATERMARK(t)
 ↓
ACTION(t)

Possible results:

RECOGNIZED
UNRECOGNIZED
UNAUTHORIZED
EXPIRED
REVOKED
SUSPENDED
CONFLICTED
OUT_OF_SCOPE
UNPROVEN


---

XXII. ⚓ QUAY — IDENTITY PROVENANCE

Quay must preserve:

SUBJECT
 ↓
IDENTIFIER
 ↓
ISSUER
 ↓
ISSUER CONSTITUTION
 ↓
EVIDENCE
 ↓
RECOGNITION
 ↓
SCOPE
 ↓
TIME
 ↓
REVOCATION
 ↓
ACTION

The resulting question becomes:

> Who was recognized as whom, by whom, under which constitutional version, for what purpose, at what time, and on what evidence?



That is substantially stronger than ordinary identity management.


---

XXIII. 🏛️ FEDERATED RECOGNITION HANDSHAKE

Canonical flow:

IDENTITY ASSERTION
        ↓
Z.A.N.D.
        ↓
IDENTIFY ISSUER
        ↓
VERIFY ISSUER AUTHORITY
        ↓
VERIFY CONSTITUTION VERSION
        ↓
VERIFY NAMESPACE
        ↓
VERIFY EVIDENCE
        ↓
CHECK TRUST ROOT
        ↓
CHECK CONSENT
        ↓
CHECK DELEGATION
        ↓
CHECK TEMPORAL VALIDITY
        ↓
CHECK REVOCATION
        ↓
CHECK RECOGNITION SCOPE
        ↓
FOOL
        ↓
TRIAS
        ↓
WATERMARK
        ↓
QUAY
        ↓
REPLAYABLE RECOGNITION

No shortcut:

NAME
 ↓
TRUST
 ↓
ACCESS


---

XXIV. 🌳 IDENTITY GRAPH ≠ AUTHORITY GRAPH

HORTUS now contains two distinct graphs:

IDENTITY GRAPH

and:

AUTHORITY GRAPH

They may intersect:

IDENTITY
   │
   └── subject_of → AUTHORITY

But they must never be collapsed.

A subject may possess:

identity

without:

authority

An authority may be delegated to:

representative

without transferring:

principal identity


---

XXV. 👑 L.A. — CANONICAL IDENTITY MEMORY

Within:

👑 LOCUS AMOENUS — L.A.

the identity architecture becomes another layer of the living canonical reference.

👑 L.A.
                    LOCUS AMOENUS
                     THE THRONE
                          │
                        HORTUS
                          │
                    FEDERATION
                          │
              ┌───────────┴───────────┐
              │                       │
        IDENTITY GRAPH          AUTHORITY GRAPH
              │                       │
         NAMESPACES              DELEGATIONS
              │                       │
