☄️🦆🌱 R7.08 — CANONICAL

And now the next boundary becomes visible.

Once a federation can establish:

WHO
↓
IS RECOGNIZED
↓
UNDER WHICH NAME
↓
BY WHOM
↓
UNDER WHICH TRUST ROOT

the inevitable next question is:

> How do federated identities communicate, delegate, transact and act across Citadels while preserving message integrity, intent, authority, provenance and constitutional scope?



That opens:

☄️🌱 R7.09 — HORTUS FEDERATED MESSAGING, INTENT, CAPABILITY, DELEGATION & CROSS-CITADEL ACTION PROTOCOL

The next boundary:

HOW CAN A RECOGNIZED ENTITY ACT ACROSS A SOVEREIGN BOUNDARY WITHOUT ITS MESSAGE BECOMING AUTHORITY BY ACCIDENT?

🦆☄️🌱👑⚓💧

☄️🌱🦆 R7.09 — HORTUS FEDERATED MESSAGING, INTENT, CAPABILITY, DELEGATION & CROSS-CITADEL ACTION PROTOCOL

Status: CANONICAL ENGINEERING BUILD

R7.00 established cross-version interoperability.
R7.01 established membership, admission, suspension, exit & succession.
R7.02 established federative governance & amendment.
R7.03 established conflict, adjudication, deadlock & remedy.
R7.04 established constitutional evolution, migration & continuity.
R7.05 established fork, merge, fragmentation & succession.
R7.06 established cross-lineage assets, obligations, evidence & liability.
R7.07 established shared infrastructure, trust anchors & common services.
R7.08 established federated identity, namespaces, recognition & trust-root interoperability.

R7.09 now crosses the next boundary:

> Recognition establishes who or what a participant is. But recognition alone does not explain what that participant is asking another Citadel to do.



A message can contain:

IDENTITY
INTENT
REQUEST
CAPABILITY
DELEGATION
AUTHORITY
SCOPE

But these must never collapse into one another.

The foundational distinction becomes:

A MESSAGE MAY CARRY AUTHORITY PROOF WITHOUT ITSELF BECOMING AUTHORITY.


---

I. 🌐 THE CROSS-CITADEL ACTION PROBLEM

Consider:

CITADEL A
   │
   │  "Please execute X"
   ↓
CITADEL B

What exactly arrived?

Was it:

MESSAGE
REQUEST
INTENT
COMMAND
DELEGATION
AUTHORIZATION

These are not equivalent.

A malicious or merely malformed system could attempt:

MESSAGE
   ↓
INTERPRET AS COMMAND
   ↓
EXECUTE

PALACO rejects this.

Instead:

MESSAGE
 ↓
PARSE
 ↓
IDENTIFY SENDER
 ↓
IDENTIFY INTENT
 ↓
VERIFY AUTHORITY
 ↓
VERIFY DELEGATION
 ↓
VERIFY CAPABILITY
 ↓
VERIFY RECEIVER POLICY
 ↓
TRIAS
 ↓
ACTION


---

II. 🌱 Z.A.N.D. — NO IMPLIED INTENT

R7.09 extends Z.A.N.D.:

MESSAGE
≠
INTENT

and:

INTENT
≠
AUTHORITY

and:

REQUEST
≠
COMMAND

and:

COMMAND
≠
AUTHORIZED ACTION

Therefore:

> The receiving Citadel SHALL NOT infer authority merely from the form, origin, confidence, urgency, or apparent intent of a message.




---

III. 🧬 THE FEDERATED MESSAGE ENVELOPE

R7.09 introduces:

FederatedMessage

Conceptually:

FederatedMessage {
    message_id
    sender_identity
    sender_citadel
    sender_constitution_version
    receiver_citadel
    receiver_constitution_version
    message_type
    intent
    requested_action
    capability_reference
    delegation_reference
    authority_reference
    scope
    temporal_bounds
    nonce
    dependencies
    evidence_reference
    response_requirements
    revocation_reference
    provenance
    watermark
}

The envelope carries claims and references.

It does not automatically establish their validity.


---

IV. 🔱 MESSAGE ≠ AUTHORITY

This becomes a core R7.09 invariant:

MESSAGE
≠
AUTHORITY

Even if:

message.sender = authorized_agent

the message still requires action-specific evaluation.

Example:

Agent A

may possess:

READ

while requesting:

DELETE

The receiving Citadel must not reason:

VALID_IDENTITY
+
VALID_MESSAGE
=
VALID_DELETE

Instead:

VALID_IDENTITY
∩
VALID_AUTHORITY
∩
VALID_SCOPE
∩
VALID_DELEGATION
∩
RECEIVER_POLICY


---

V. 🎯 INTENT AS A FIRST-CLASS OBJECT

R7.09 introduces:

Intent

Conceptually:

Intent {
    intent_id
    actor
    objective
    requested_effect
    target
    scope
    constraints
    temporal_bounds
    dependencies
    evidence
}

Intent describes:

> What the actor is trying to accomplish.



It does not establish:

> Whether the actor is constitutionally permitted to accomplish it.



Therefore:

INTENT ≠ AUTHORIZATION


---

VI. 🧭 INTENT VS EFFECT

An actor might request:

"Verify this document."

But the technical operation might cause:

READ
PARSE
STORE
TRANSMIT

The constitutional system must evaluate the actual effects, not merely the friendly wording of the intent.

Thus:

DECLARED_INTENT
+
ACTUAL_EFFECTS

must be compared.

If:

INTENT = READ
EFFECT = EXPORT

then:

MISMATCH

must be surfaced.


---

VII. 🚨 NO HIDDEN EFFECTS

R7.09 therefore establishes:

> A cross-Citadel action SHALL NOT acquire unexamined authority through side effects that were absent from the declared action scope.



Conceptually:

DECLARED_SCOPE
        ↓
EXPECTED_EFFECTS
        ↓
ACTUAL_EFFECTS
        ↓
COMPARE

Possible outcome:

MATCH
MISMATCH
EXPANDED_EFFECT
UNKNOWN_EFFECT
UNPROVEN


---

VIII. ⚙️ CAPABILITY TOKENS

R7.09 introduces bounded:

FederatedCapability

Conceptually:

FederatedCapability {
    capability_id
    issuer
    subject
    target
    operation
    scope
    constraints
    validity_period
    delegation_chain
    revocation_reference
    evidence_reference
    provenance
    watermark
}

A capability is not a general permission.

It is:

BOUNDED_PROVEN CAPABILITY


---

IX. 🔒 CAPABILITY IS NOT IDENTITY

An entity may be:

RECOGNIZED

without possessing:

CAPABILITY

Likewise:

CAPABILITY

does not establish:

IDENTITY

The system therefore keeps:

IDENTITY
CAPABILITY
AUTHORITY
DELEGATION

as separate constitutional objects.


---

X. 🧬 CAPABILITY INTERSECTION

For cross-Citadel execution:

CAP_A
∩
AUTH_B
∩
FEDERATION_SCOPE
∩
ACTION_SCOPE
∩
TIME

produces:

VALID_EXECUTION_SCOPE

Therefore:

FEDERATED_CAPABILITY ≤ PROVEN_INTERSECTION

No capability may expand merely because it crosses a federation boundary.


---

XI. ⚖️ DELEGATED CAPABILITY

A capability may arise from delegation:

PRINCIPAL
   ↓
DELEGATES
   ↓
AGENT
   ↓
REQUEST
   ↓
RECEIVER

The receiver must reconstruct:

PRINCIPAL_AUTHORITY
 ↓
DELEGATION
 ↓
SUB-DELEGATION
 ↓
CAPABILITY
 ↓
ACTION

The chain must remain intact.


---

XII. 🔗 DELEGATION CHAIN INTEGRITY

Suppose:

A delegates X → B
B delegates X → C
C requests X → D

R7.09 requires:

A
↓
B
↓
C
↓
D

to preserve:

origin
scope
time
conditions
revocation

A sub-delegate cannot silently transform:

READ

into:

WRITE

or:

WRITE

into:

EXECUTE

Therefore:

SUBDELEGATED_SCOPE ≤ ORIGINATING_SCOPE


---

XIII. 🦆 FOOL — MESSAGE ADVERSARY

FOOL attacks the communication boundary.

It asks:

Is the sender really who it claims to be?

Was the identity assertion valid at this time?

Was the message altered?

Was the intent altered?

Is the capability genuine?

Was the capability revoked?

Was the delegation still active?

Did a sub-delegate exceed its scope?

Is the receiver authorized to perform the requested operation?

Does the message conceal side effects?

Is replay being attempted?

Was the message intended for another Citadel?

Does the requested operation exceed the narrowest boundary?

And:

Does the message prove what it claims to prove—or merely assert it?


---

XIV. 🧠 BRAINPOWER — INTENT ANALYSIS

BRAINPOWER receives:

MESSAGE
+
IDENTITY
+
INTENT
+
CAPABILITY
+
DELEGATION
+
CONSTITUTIONS
+
FEDERATION CONTRACT
+
TARGET POLICY

It can construct:

INTENT GRAPH
AUTHORITY GRAPH
CAPABILITY GRAPH
DEPENDENCY GRAPH
EFFECT GRAPH

and identify:

COMPATIBLE
AMBIGUOUS
OVERBROAD
MISSING_AUTHORITY
MISSING_DELEGATION
EFFECT_MISMATCH
REPLAY
CONFLICT
UNPROVEN

But:

BRAINPOWER
≠
EXECUTION AUTHORITY


---

XV. ⚖️ TRIAS — ACTION DETERMINATION

TRIAS evaluates:

WHO
WHAT
WHY
WHERE
WHEN
UNDER WHICH CONSTITUTION
UNDER WHICH AUTHORITY
UNDER WHICH DELEGATION
UNDER WHICH CAPABILITY
WITH WHICH EFFECTS
UNDER WHICH FEDERATION RULE

Possible outcomes:

AUTHORIZED
AUTHORIZED_WITH_SCOPE
DENIED
OUT_OF_SCOPE
DELEGATION_INVALID
CAPABILITY_INVALID
IDENTITY_UNRECOGNIZED
EFFECT_MISMATCH
EXPIRED
REVOKED
CONFLICTED
UNPROVEN


---

XVI. 🔐 MESSAGE INTEGRITY

The protocol must distinguish:

AUTHENTICITY

from:

AUTHORIZATION

A cryptographically authentic message may still be unauthorized.

Therefore:

VALID_SIGNATURE
≠
VALID_AUTHORITY

Likewise:

VALID_IDENTITY
≠
VALID_MESSAGE_INTENT

And:

VALID_MESSAGE
≠
VALID_ACTION

This creates a layered verification model.


---

XVII. 💧 WATERMARKED MESSAGE LINEAGE

Every material cross-Citadel message receives:

W_SENDER
W_IDENTITY
W_INTENT
W_AUTHORITY
W_DELEGATION
W_CAPABILITY
W_RECEIVER
W_SCOPE
W_TIME
W_EFFECT
W_TRIAS

Thus:

MESSAGE
=
PROVENANCE-BEARING ACTION REQUEST

The watermark must preserve the difference between:

WHO SPOKE

and:

WHO AUTHORIZED

and:

WHO EXECUTED


---

XVIII. ⚓ QUAY — MESSAGE PROVENANCE

Quay records:

SENDER
 ↓
IDENTITY
 ↓
MESSAGE
 ↓
INTENT
 ↓
AUTHORITY
 ↓
DELEGATION
 ↓
CAPABILITY
 ↓
RECEIVER
 ↓
POLICY
 ↓
TRIAS
 ↓
ACTION
 ↓
RESULT

The question becomes:

> Why did Citadel B execute—or refuse—the request from Citadel A?



And the verifier can reconstruct the answer.


---

XIX. 🧪 R5 — FEDERATED MESSAGE REPLAY

R5 now supports:

REPLAY MESSAGE AT T

The verifier reconstructs:

SENDER_STATE(t)
 ↓
IDENTITY_STATE(t)
 ↓
MESSAGE_STATE(t)
 ↓
INTENT(t)
 ↓
