☄️🦆🌱 R7.09 — CANONICAL

And the next boundary is now visible.

We can now establish:

WHO
↓
IS RECOGNIZED
↓
WHO SENT THE MESSAGE
↓
WHAT THEY INTEND
↓
WHAT THEY ARE CAPABLE OF
↓
WHAT THEY ARE AUTHORIZED TO REQUEST
↓
WHETHER THE RECEIVER MAY ACT

But one major constitutional problem remains:

> What happens when a federated action is not merely a request, but produces a consequential effect in another Citadel—changing state, creating obligations, consuming resources, triggering agents, or initiating further autonomous actions?



That opens:

☄️🌱 R7.10 — HORTUS FEDERATED TRANSACTION, ATOMICITY, COMMITMENT, EFFECT & CROSS-CITADEL STATE TRANSITION PROTOCOL

The next boundary is:

WHEN A CROSS-CITADEL ACTION CHANGES REALITY, WHO OWNS THE RESULTING STATE?

And that is where message interoperability becomes state interoperability. 🦆☄️🌱⚓💧👑

☄️🦆🌱 R7.10 — HORTUS FEDERATED TRANSACTION, ATOMICITY, COMMITMENT, EFFECT & CROSS-CITADEL STATE TRANSITION PROTOCOL

Status: CANONICAL ENGINEERING BUILD

R7.00 established cross-version interoperability.
R7.01 established membership, admission, suspension, exit & succession.
R7.02 established federative governance & amendment.
R7.03 established conflict, adjudication, deadlock & remedy.
R7.04 established constitutional evolution, migration & continuity.
R7.05 established fork, merge, fragmentation & succession.
R7.06 established cross-lineage assets, obligations, evidence & liability.
R7.07 established shared infrastructure, trust anchors & common services.
R7.08 established federated identity, namespaces, recognition & trust-root interoperability.
R7.09 established messaging, intent, capability, delegation & cross-Citadel action requests.

R7.10 now crosses the decisive boundary:

> An action is no longer merely a message. It changes state.



That creates a fundamentally different constitutional problem.

CITADEL A
    │
    │ REQUEST
    ↓
CITADEL B
    │
    │ ACTION
    ↓
STATE CHANGE

Who owns that state change?

Who is responsible for it?

What happens if A commits but B fails?

What happens if B commits but A loses connectivity?

What happens if the constitutional state changes between authorization and execution?

And most importantly:

HOW CAN MULTIPLE SOVEREIGN CITADELS CHANGE STATE TOGETHER WITHOUT CREATING A SHARED SOVEREIGN STATE?

The answer is:

FEDERATED STATE TRANSITION WITHOUT SOVEREIGNTY COLLAPSE.


---

I. 🌐 THE TRANSACTION PROBLEM

R7.09 established:

REQUEST
 ↓
AUTHORITY
 ↓
CAPABILITY
 ↓
TRIAS
 ↓
ACTION

R7.10 adds:

ACTION
 ↓
EFFECT
 ↓
STATE TRANSITION

Therefore:

ACTION
≠
STATE

and:

AUTHORIZED ACTION
≠
SUCCESSFUL STATE TRANSITION

A system can be authorized to attempt an action while the actual state transition fails.

That distinction must be preserved.


---

II. 🌱 Z.A.N.D. — NO IMPLIED COMMITMENT

R7.10 extends Z.A.N.D.:

REQUEST
≠
COMMITMENT

AUTHORIZATION
≠
EXECUTION

EXECUTION
≠
SUCCESS

SUCCESS
≠
GLOBAL COMMITMENT

Therefore:

> No Citadel SHALL infer that another Citadel has committed to a state transition merely because it authorized, acknowledged, prepared, or attempted an action.




---

III. 🧬 FEDERATED TRANSACTION

R7.10 introduces:

FederatedTransaction

Conceptually:

FederatedTransaction {
    transaction_id
    participants
    originating_citadel
    target_citadels
    constitutional_versions
    transaction_scope
    requested_effects
    authority_basis
    delegation_chain
    capability_set
    preconditions
    dependencies
    prepare_rules
    commit_rules
    abort_rules
    timeout_rules
    compensation_rules
    conflict_rules
    temporal_bounds
    evidence_requirements
    provenance
    watermark
}

The transaction is a bounded coordination object.

It does not create a new sovereign constitution.


---

IV. 🔱 LOCAL STATE REMAINS LOCAL

Suppose:

A → STATE_A
B → STATE_B

A federated transaction may coordinate:

STATE_A(t)
+
STATE_B(t)

But it does not create:

STATE_FEDERATION

unless the federation explicitly maintains a separate constitutional state of its own.

Therefore:

FEDERATED COORDINATION ≠ FEDERATED OWNERSHIP OF MEMBER STATE

Each Citadel remains authoritative over its own state.


---

V. 🏛️ STATE TRANSITION OWNERSHIP

For every resulting state transition:

STATE_CHANGE

must identify:

originating_authority
executing_citadel
affected_state_owner
constitutional_basis
transaction_id
causal_predecessor

Thus:

A requests
B executes
B's state changes

does not mean:

A owns B's state

Nor does:

FEDERATION coordinates

mean:

FEDERATION owns resulting state


---

VI. ⚖️ TRANSACTIONAL AUTHORITY

The system distinguishes:

AUTHORITY_TO_REQUEST
AUTHORITY_TO_EXECUTE
AUTHORITY_TO_COMMIT
AUTHORITY_TO_ABORT
AUTHORITY_TO_COMPENSATE

These may belong to different constitutional actors.

Example:

A
 └── authorized to request X

B
 └── authorized to execute X

B
 └── authorized to commit X

The requester's authority does not automatically become executor authority.


---

VII. 🎯 PRECONDITIONS

A transaction may require:

PRECONDITION_1
PRECONDITION_2
PRECONDITION_3

Before execution:

VERIFY ALL PRECONDITIONS

If one fails:

TRANSACTION
 ↓
BLOCKED

rather than:

PARTIAL GUESS

This creates:

CONSTITUTIONAL PRECONDITION GATE


---

VIII. 🧭 SNAPSHOT AUTHORIZATION

Authorization must be evaluated against a defined state.

Conceptually:

AUTHORIZATION_STATE = STATE(t_authorization)

But execution may occur at:

t_execution

If:

t_authorization ≠ t_execution

the system must determine whether authorization remains valid.

Therefore:

AUTHORIZED_AT_T1

does not automatically mean:

AUTHORIZED_AT_T2


---

IX. ⏳ AUTHORIZATION-TO-COMMIT WINDOW

R7.10 introduces:

AUTHORIZATION_WINDOW

Conceptually:

T_AUTH
   │
   ├── VALID
   │
   ├── PREPARE
   │
   ├── COMMIT
   │
T_EXPIRY

If the authorization expires before commit:

COMMIT = INVALID

unless an explicit constitutional mechanism permits continuation.


---

X. 🧪 PREPARE ≠ COMMIT

A critical distinction:

PREPARE

means:

> The participant has verified that it can perform the transaction under current conditions.



It does not mean:

COMMITTED

Therefore:

PREPARED
≠
COMMITTED

This becomes fundamental for replay.


---

XI. ⚓ FEDERATED TWO-PHASE CONSTITUTIONAL MODEL

R7.10 may use a conceptual two-stage protocol:

PHASE I
PREPARE

followed by:

PHASE II
COMMIT

But unlike ordinary distributed transaction systems, PALACO requires each phase to remain constitutionally accountable.

REQUEST
 ↓
PRECONDITIONS
 ↓
AUTHORITY
 ↓
PREPARE
 ↓
TRIAS
 ↓
COMMIT
 ↓
STATE

The protocol itself cannot bypass local constitutional authority.


---

XII. 🚨 THE PARTIAL-COMMIT PROBLEM

Suppose:

A → PREPARED
B → PREPARED

A → COMMITTED
B → FAILED

We now have:

PARTIAL STATE TRANSITION

PALACO cannot simply declare:

FEDERATION SUCCESS

because the federation does not vote reality into existence.

Instead:

COMMIT STATUS
+
PARTICIPANT STATES
+
FAILURE EVIDENCE

must be evaluated.

Possible outcome:

COMMITTED_ALL
PARTIAL_COMMIT
ABORTED_ALL
COMPENSATION_REQUIRED
INCONSISTENT
UNRESOLVED


---

XIII. 🧬 ATOMICITY IS NOT AUTOMATIC

R7.10 makes an important distinction:

TRANSACTIONAL INTENT
≠
ATOMIC REALITY

A transaction can only be considered atomic if:

ATOMICITY

is explicitly supported and proven by the participating systems.

Therefore:

> The existence of a federated transaction does not itself guarantee atomic execution.




---

XIV. 🔄 COMPENSATING ACTION

When atomic rollback is impossible:

A committed
B failed

the protocol may define:

COMPENSATING_ACTION

But compensation is itself a new constitutional action.

Therefore:

FAILURE
 ↓
COMPENSATION_REQUEST
 ↓
AUTHORITY
 ↓
TRIAS
 ↓
COMPENSATING_ACTION

A compensation mechanism cannot silently invent rollback authority.


---

XV. 🦆 FOOL — TRANSACTION CHALLENGE

FOOL attacks the state transition boundary:

Was every participant correctly identified?

Was the correct constitutional version used?

Was authorization still valid at commit?

Did a delegation expire?

Did a participant revoke authority?

Were all preconditions satisfied?

Did the actual effects match the requested effects?

Was one participant committed while another failed?

Was compensation actually authorized?

Did the transaction exceed federation scope?

Was state mutated before constitutional determination?

Can the resulting state be reconstructed?

And the decisive question:

WHO PROVED THAT THIS STATE TRANSITION WAS ALLOWED TO OCCUR?


---

XVI. 🧠 BRAINPOWER — TRANSACTION ANALYSIS

BRAINPOWER constructs:

TRANSACTION GRAPH

containing:

REQUEST
 ↓
PARTICIPANTS
 ↓
PRECONDITIONS
 ↓
AUTHORITIES
 ↓
CAPABILITIES
 ↓
PREPARE
 ↓
COMMIT
 ↓
EFFECT
 ↓
STATE

It can detect:

DEPENDENCY_FAILURE
PARTIAL_COMMIT
STALE_AUTHORIZATION
SCOPE_EXPANSION
STATE_DIVERGENCE
CONFLICT
MISSING_COMPENSATION
UNPROVEN_COMMIT

But again:

BRAINPOWER
≠
COMMIT AUTHORITY


---

XVII. ⚖️ TRIAS — COMMIT DETERMINATION

TRIAS evaluates each transition:

AUTHORITY
+
VERSION
+
SCOPE
+
TIME
+
PRECONDITIONS
+
CAPABILITY
+
DELEGATION
+
TRANSACTION_RULES
+
ACTUAL_EFFECT

Then determines:

COMMIT_VALID
COMMIT_INVALID
COMMIT_BLOCKED
COMMIT_EXPIRED
COMMIT_REVOKED
PARTIAL_COMMIT
COMPENSATION_REQUIRED
UNPROVEN


---

XVIII. 💧 WATERMARKED TRANSACTION

The transaction receives a causal watermark:

W_TX
W_REQUEST
W_AUTHORITY_A
W_AUTHORITY_B
W_DELEGATION
W_CAPABILITY
W_PREPARE
W_TRIAS
W_COMMIT
W_EFFECT
W_STATE
W_COMPENSATION

The resulting state must be attributable to the exact causal chain.

Therefore:

STATE
 ↓
WATERMARK
 ↓
CAUSE

becomes first-class.


---

XIX. ⚓ QUAY — STATE PROVENANCE

Quay now preserves:

REQUEST
 ↓
AUTHORITY
 ↓
DELEGATION
 ↓
CAPABILITY
 ↓
PRECONDITION
 ↓
PREPARE
 ↓
COMMIT
 ↓
EFFECT
 ↓
STATE_TRANSITION
 ↓
WATERMARK

The key question becomes:

> Why does this state exist?



And the verifier can answer:

WHO
WHAT
WHEN
UNDER WHICH CONSTITUTION
UNDER WHICH AUTHORITY
UNDER WHICH TRANSACTION
WITH WHICH PRECONDITIONS
WITH WHICH EFFECT


---

XX. 🧪 R5 — FEDERATED STATE REPLAY

R5 now reaches a new level.

It must reconstruct:

FEDERATED_STATE(t)

from:

STATE_A(t)
STATE_B(t)
...
STATE_N(t)

and:

TRANSACTION_STATE(t)

The replay chain:

STATE_A(t)
 ↓
STATE_B(t)
 ↓
FEDERATION_STATE(t)
 ↓
IDENTITY(t)
 ↓
AUTHORITY(t)
 ↓
DELEGATION(t)
 ↓
CAPABILITY(t)
 ↓
TRANSACTION(t)
 ↓
PRECONDITIONS(t)
 ↓
PREPARE(t)
 ↓
TRIAS(t)
 ↓
COMMIT(t)
 ↓
EFFECT(t)
 ↓
STATE_TRANSITION(t)


---

XXI. 🧬 STATE LINEAGE

R7.10 introduces:

STATE_LINEAGE

Conceptually:

StateLineage {
    state_id
    parent_state
    transition_id
    transaction_id
    actor
    authority
    constitutional_version
    timestamp
    effects
    evidence
    watermark
}

This turns state itself into a provenance object.


---

XXII. 🌳 STATE ≠ EVIDENCE

A state may say:

BALANCE = 100

but the system must separately preserve:

WHY_BALANCE_IS_100

Therefore:

STATE
≠
PROOF_OF_STATE

Quay maintains the relationship:

STATE
 ↕
EVIDENCE

so that a verifier can reconstruct the causal transition.


---

XXIII. 🚨 CONSTITUTIONAL STATE DIVERGENCE

Suppose:

A believes:
TRANSACTION = COMMITTED

B believes:
TRANSACTION = ABORTED

The federation cannot simply choose:

MAJORITY = TRUE

Instead:

DIVERGENCE
 ↓
COLLECT EVIDENCE
 ↓
RECONSTRUCT STATES
 ↓
APPLY TRANSACTION RULE
 ↓
TRIAS

Possible outcomes:

A_COMMITTED_B_ABORTED
RECONCILED
COMPENSATION_REQUIRED
CONFLICTED
UNPROVEN


---

XXIV. 🔱 NO STATE MAJORITY

R7.10 strengthens the R7 principle:

> A majority of Citadels cannot manufacture a state transition that did not occur under the applicable constitutional rules.



Therefore:

CONSENSUS
≠
STATE FACT

and:

VOTE
≠
COMMIT

Consensus may coordinate.

It cannot rewrite causal reality.


---

XXV. 🏛️ LOCAL COMMIT AUTHORITY

Each Citadel retains the final authority over its own local state.

Thus:

FEDERATION
    ↓
REQUEST COMMIT

cannot automatically become:

FEDERATION
    ↓
FORCE LOCAL STATE CHANGE

Instead:

FEDERATION
 ↓
COORDINATE
 ↓
LOCAL CONSTITUTION
 ↓
LOCAL TRIAS
 ↓
LOCAL COMMIT

This is the architectural firewall against federation becoming a super-Citadel.


---

XXVI. 🌐 FEDERATED COMMITMENT

A federated transaction may establish:

COMMITMENT RELATIONSHIP

between participants.

For example:

A commits X
only if
B commits Y

But that dependency must itself be explicit.

Conceptually:

CommitCondition {
    participant
    required_state
    required_transition
    temporal_bound
    evidence_requirement
}


---

XXVII. 🔒 CROSS-CITADEL PRECONDITION

A Citadel may require:

B_STATE = VERIFIED

before:

A_COMMIT

But A must not simply trust B's assertion.

It requires:

B_STATE
 ↓
EVIDENCE
 ↓
WATERMARK
 ↓
TRIAS

Thus:

STATE ASSERTIONS CROSS BOUNDARIES ONLY AS PROVEN STATE CLAIMS.


---

XXVIII. 🌱 THE NARROWEST COMMITMENT

R7.10 extends the R7.00 intersection principle:

COMMIT_SCOPE
=
AUTHORITY_A
∩
AUTHORITY_B
∩
FEDERATION_SCOPE
∩
TRANSACTION_SCOPE
∩
TEMPORAL_SCOPE
∩
PRECONDITIONS

Therefore:

FEDERATED_COMMITMENT
≤
PROVEN_INTERSECTION


---

XXIX. 🔄 REVOCATION DURING TRANSACTION

Suppose:

10:00
A authorizes X

10:02
transaction prepares

10:03
A revokes X

10:04
commit requested

The system cannot blindly commit.

It must evaluate:

REVOCATION
 ↓
TRANSACTION_STATE
 ↓
COMMIT_RULES
 ↓
TRIAS

Possible outcomes:

COMMIT_ALLOWED
COMMIT_BLOCKED
REVALIDATION_REQUIRED
COMPENSATION_REQUIRED
UNPROVEN

The answer depends on the explicit constitutional semantics of the transaction.


---

XXX. 🕰️ HISTORICAL STATE

R5 must distinguish:

CURRENT_STATE

from:

HISTORICAL_STATE

Thus:

ACTION_AT_10:00

must be evaluated against:

STATE(10:00)

not:

STATE(NOW)

This makes historical federated state a first-class PALACO primitive.


---

