R7.14 — HORTUS FEDERATED TRUST ROOTS, ATTESTATION CHAINS, KEY ROTATION, CRYPTOGRAPHIC IDENTITY & ROOT-OF-TRUST SUCCESSION

Want nu ontstaat de diepste technische vraag van R7:

> Als identiteit, bevoegdheid, bewijs, herstel en re-admission allemaal cryptografisch aantoonbaar moeten zijn — wie of wat mag dan uiteindelijk de cryptografische trust anchor zijn, en hoe kan zelfs die trust anchor worden vervangen zonder de constitutionele geschiedenis te breken?



Daar ontmoeten cryptographic provenance, key succession, constitutional succession en R5 replay elkaar.

GO ☄️🦆🌱

☄️🦆🌱 R7.14 — HORTUS FEDERATED TRUST ROOTS, ATTESTATION CHAINS, KEY ROTATION, CRYPTOGRAPHIC IDENTITY & ROOT-OF-TRUST SUCCESSION

Status: CANONICAL ENGINEERING BUILD
Review constellation: MENTOR · TRIAS · ORACLE · BRAINPOWER · FOOL
Canonical reference: 👑 LOCUS AMOENUS — L.A.

R7.13 established:

> A Citadel may return only through proven continuity, revalidated authority and explicit constitutional re-admission.



R7.14 now reaches beneath identity itself.

The next question is unavoidable:

> What proves the identity of the entity making the claim?



The answer cannot be:

CURRENT KEY
↓
TRUST

because keys rotate, expire, are compromised, are replaced, and sometimes must be revoked.

Therefore R7.14 establishes:

IDENTITY IS A CRYPTOGRAPHIC LINEAGE, NOT A SINGLE KEY.


---

I. 🔐 THE TRUST-ROOT PROBLEM

A federated Citadel may possess:

ROOT KEY
IDENTITY KEY
AUTHORIZATION KEY
SESSION KEY
ATTESTATION KEY
SIGNING KEY

But:

KEY ≠ IDENTITY

A key is an instrument through which identity can be evidenced.

Therefore:

IDENTITY
 ↓
KEY LINEAGE
 ↓
ATTESTATION
 ↓
CONSTITUTIONAL STATE
 ↓
PROVENANCE

must remain distinguishable.


---

II. 🌱 Z.A.N.D. — NO IMPLIED CRYPTOGRAPHIC TRUST

R7.14 establishes:

NEW_KEY
≠
NEW_IDENTITY

NEW_KEY
≠
AUTOMATIC_TRUST

OLD_KEY
≠
AUTOMATICALLY_VALID

VALID_SIGNATURE
≠
VALID_AUTHORITY

A cryptographically authentic message can still be constitutionally unauthorized.

Therefore:

CRYPTOGRAPHIC AUTHENTICITY IS NECESSARY BUT NOT SUFFICIENT FOR CONSTITUTIONAL AUTHORITY.


---

III. 🧬 THE KEY-LINEAGE MODEL

R7.14 introduces:

KEY_LINEAGE

Conceptually:

K₀
 ↓
K₁
 ↓
K₂
 ↓
K₃

Every transition must explain:

WHO
WHAT
WHY
WHEN
UNDER WHICH AUTHORITY
UNDER WHICH CONSTITUTION
WITH WHICH EVIDENCE

A key rotation is therefore itself a constitutional event.


---

IV. 💧 WATERMARKED KEY SUCCESSION

Each key transition receives provenance:

W_KEY {
    key_id
    predecessor
    successor
    purpose
    issuer
    authorization
    constitutional_version
    effective_time
    expiry_time
    revocation_reference
    attestation_reference
}

Thus:

K₂

does not merely say:

> “I am the new key.”



It can demonstrate:

> “I succeeded K₁ through this authorized transition, under this constitutional state, at this time.”




---

V. ⚖️ KEY ROTATION ≠ AUTHORITY TRANSFER

This distinction becomes canonical.

KEY ROTATION

means:

cryptographic instrument changed

It does not necessarily mean:

constitutional authority changed

Conversely:

AUTHORITY TRANSFER

does not necessarily mean:

same cryptographic key

Therefore both transitions must be independently represented.


---

VI. 🏛️ TRUST ROOTS

A Citadel may maintain a root-of-trust structure:

ROOT
                  │
          ┌───────┴───────┐
          │               │
      IDENTITY          ATTESTATION
          │               │
       AUTHORITY        EVIDENCE
          │               │
       SESSION          ACTION

But the root itself cannot be treated as constitutionally immortal.

Therefore:

EVERY TRUST ROOT MUST HAVE A SUCCESSION PATH.


---

VII. ☄️ ROOT SUCCESSION

Suppose:

ROOT₁

must be replaced.

The federation cannot simply observe:

ROOT₂ EXISTS

and conclude:

ROOT₂ = ROOT₁

Instead:

ROOT₁
 ↓
SUCCESSION AUTHORITY
 ↓
SUCCESSION EVIDENCE
 ↓
ROOT₂
 ↓
TRIAS
 ↓
WATERMARK

produces:

ROOT_SUCCESSION_PROOF


---

VIII. 🚨 THE ROOT COMPROMISE CASE

The hardest case:

ROOT₁ COMPROMISED

Now the old root cannot safely authorize its own replacement.

R7.14 therefore requires a pre-established constitutional recovery mechanism.

Possible anchors include:

MULTI-PARTY RECOVERY AUTHORITY
OFFLINE RECOVERY AUTHORITY
HARDWARE-ANCHORED ATTESTATION
FEDERATION RECOVERY PROTOCOL
CONSTITUTIONALLY DEFINED SUCCESSOR

But none receives authority merely because it exists.

Its authority must itself be proven.


---

IX. 🔱 CONSTITUTIONAL ROOT OF TRUST

R7.14 introduces:

CONSTITUTIONAL_ROOT_OF_TRUST

This is not necessarily a cryptographic key.

It is the combination of:

CONSTITUTION
+
AUTHORIZED SUCCESSION MECHANISM
+
IDENTITY LINEAGE
+
EVIDENCE ROOT
+
TEMPORAL RULES

Thus:

CRYPTOGRAPHIC ROOT
⊂
CONSTITUTIONAL TRUST MODEL

not:

CRYPTOGRAPHIC ROOT
=
CONSTITUTION


---

X. 🔮 ORACLE — TRUST-CHAIN ANALYSIS

ORACLE evaluates:

ROOT
 ↓
KEY
 ↓
ATTESTATION
 ↓
SIGNATURE
 ↓
EVIDENCE

and asks:

IS THE CHAIN COMPLETE?
ARE ANY LINKS REVOKED?
ARE ANY LINKS EXPIRED?
IS THERE A GAP?
IS THE SUCCESSION AUTHORIZED?
IS THE TIMESTAMP VALID?
DO MULTIPLE SOURCES CONFLICT?

Output:

TRUST_CHAIN_VALID
TRUST_CHAIN_PARTIAL
TRUST_CHAIN_BROKEN
TRUST_CHAIN_CONFLICTED
TRUST_CHAIN_UNPROVEN


---

XI. 🧠 BRAINPOWER — CRYPTOGRAPHIC LINEAGE REASONING

BRAINPOWER reconstructs:

WHO HELD WHICH KEY
AT WHAT TIME
FOR WHICH PURPOSE
UNDER WHICH AUTHORITY

It must distinguish:

KEY POSSESSION

from:

KEY AUTHORIZATION

and:

SIGNATURE VALIDITY

from:

ACTION VALIDITY

This prevents a cryptographically correct but constitutionally invalid action from becoming “valid” merely because its signature verifies.


---

XII. 🦆 FOOL — TRUST-ROOT ATTACK

FOOL attacks the trust chain:

> Who authorized the root?



> Who authorized its successor?



> Could a compromised key authorize itself?



> Was a revoked key used after revocation?



> Was a new key backdated?



> Can two different roots both claim succession?



> Does a signature prove identity, or merely possession of a private key?



And the deepest question:

WHO AUTHORIZED THE AUTHORIZER?

The answer must terminate in an explicit constitutional trust mechanism.

Never in assumption.


---

XIII. ⚖️ TRIAS — TRUST DETERMINATION

TRIAS determines:

ROOT VALID
ROOT SUCCESSION VALID
KEY VALID
KEY SUCCESSION VALID
ATTESTATION VALID
CHAIN VALID
CHAIN UNPROVEN
CHAIN REJECTED

TRIAS does not generate cryptographic proof.

It determines the constitutional meaning of the available proof.


---

XIV. 🧪 R5 — TRUST-CHAIN REPLAY

R5 must reconstruct historical cryptographic validity.

Example:

10:00 ROOT₁ VALID
11:00 KEY₁ VALID
12:00 KEY₁ COMPROMISED
12:05 KEY₁ REVOKED
12:10 ROOT SUCCESSION INITIATED
12:20 ROOT₂ ATTESTED
12:30 KEY₂ ISSUED
13:00 FEDERATION RE-ADMISSION

A verifier querying:

12:03

must not use the state at:

13:00

Therefore:

TRUST_STATE(t)

becomes a first-class replay object.


---

XV. ⏳ TEMPORAL KEY VALIDITY

Every security-relevant key relationship must be evaluated against time:

VALID_FROM
VALID_UNTIL
REVOCATION_TIME
SUCCESSION_TIME
ATTESTATION_TIME

Hence:

SIGNATURE_VALID_NOW

does not necessarily imply:

SIGNATURE_VALID_AT_ACTION_TIME

This is essential for forensic replay.


---

XVI. 🚫 NO BACKDATED TRUST

R7.14 establishes:

> A later-created trust relationship SHALL NOT retroactively legitimize an earlier action unless the applicable constitutional mechanism explicitly permits such recognition.



Thus:

NEW_ROOT
↓
cannot automatically rewrite
↓
OLD_HISTORY

History remains history.


---

XVII. 💧 WATERMARK — CRYPTOGRAPHIC PROVENANCE

A federated action now carries:

W_IDENTITY
W_ROOT
W_KEY
W_ATTESTATION
W_AUTHORITY
W_CONSENT
W_DELEGATION
W_SCOPE
W_TIME
W_FEDERATION
W_DETERMINATION
W_ACTION

This creates:

CRYPTOGRAPHIC + CONSTITUTIONAL MULTI-PROVENANCE


---

XVIII. ⚓ QUAY — TRUST-LINEAGE STORAGE

Quay preserves:

ROOT
 ↓
SUCCESSION
 ↓
KEY
 ↓
ATTESTATION
 ↓
AUTHORITY
 ↓
ACTION
 ↓
EVIDENCE

A verifier can therefore ask:

> Which root ultimately supports this action?



and:

> Was that root constitutionally valid when the action occurred?




---

XIX. 🌐 FEDERATION TRUST

A federation must not maintain only:

TRUSTED CITADEL A
TRUSTED CITADEL B

It needs a richer model:

CITADEL
 ↓
IDENTITY
 ↓
ROOT
 ↓
KEY LINEAGE
 ↓
ATTESTATION
 ↓
CONSTITUTION
 ↓
AUTHORITY
 ↓
FEDERATION MEMBERSHIP

Therefore federation trust becomes:

CHAINED AND CONTEXTUAL

rather than binary.


---

XX. 🧬 CROSS-CITADEL ROOT DIFFERENCE

Citadel A:

ROOT_A

Citadel B:

ROOT_B

does not imply:

ROOT_A = ROOT_B

Nor does federation membership require a shared cryptographic root.

Instead:

ROOT_A
   │
   ├── A constitution
   │
   └── A authority

ROOT_B
   │
   ├── B constitution
   │
   └── B authority

        ↓

FEDERATION TRUST CONTRACT
        ↓
CROSS-VERSION VALIDATION

This preserves the R7.00 principle:

INTEROPERABILITY WITHOUT SOVEREIGNTY COLLAPSE.


---

XXI. 🏛️ FEDERATION ROOT ≠ SUPER-CITADEL ROOT

A federation may have infrastructure keys.

It may sign:

FEDERATION CONTRACT
FEDERATION MEMBERSHIP
FEDERATION MESSAGES
FEDERATION ATTESTATIONS

But:

> A federation cryptographic root SHALL NOT automatically become a constitutional root over member Citadels.



This is now explicit.

FED_ROOT
≠
MEMBER_CONSTITUTION


---

XXII. 🌸 HARUM — HUMAN TRUST VIEW

HARUM should never force users to interpret cryptographic lineage manually.

Instead:

> Identity verified.



> Current signing key is a verified successor of the previous key.



> The previous key was revoked at 12:05.



> No actions were accepted from that key after revocation.



> Federation trust is currently valid.



> One historical trust gap remains under review.



That is human comprehension without surrendering forensic precision.


---

XXIII. 🧑‍🏫 MENTOR — TRUST ARCHITECTURE REVIEW

Mentor asks:

CAN AN ENGINEER TRACE THE ROOT?
CAN AN OPERATOR UNDERSTAND KEY ROTATION?
CAN A VERIFIER REPLAY SUCCESSION?
CAN A SECURITY TEAM IDENTIFY THE COMPROMISE WINDOW?
CAN A FEDERATION DISTINGUISH ITS OWN ROOT FROM MEMBER ROOTS?
CAN THE SYSTEM RECOVER IF THE ROOT ITSELF FAILS?

The Mentor rule:

A TRUST MODEL THAT CANNOT EXPLAIN ITS OWN ROOT IS NOT OPERATIONALLY COMPLETE.


