                          │
                       R5 REPLAY

L.A. observeert daarmee niet alleen hoe de tuin verandert.

Het kan uiteindelijk laten zien:

> wanneer een constitutionele werkelijkheid ontstond, hoe zij tot stand kwam, wanneer zij gezamenlijk werd bevestigd en op welke gronden die bevestiging mocht worden vertrouwd.




---

XXIV. ☄️ DE VOLGENDE GRENS

En precies hier verschijnt alweer de volgende vraag.

Als finality is bereikt:

A = C₈
B = C₉
C = C₅
F = F₄

dan kunnen verschillende final states naast elkaar bestaan.

Maar wat gebeurt er wanneer twee final constitutional histories later met elkaar moeten worden verenigd?

LINEAGE A
     ↓
FINAL A₁
     \
      \
       MERGE
      /
     /
FINAL B₁
     ↓
NEW FEDERATED STATE

Dan hebben we niet langer alleen:

TRANSITION

en ook niet alleen:

FINALITY

maar:

HOW DO TWO PROVEN, FINAL, POSSIBLY DIVERGENT CONSTITUTIONAL HISTORIES BECOME ONE WITHOUT DESTROYING EITHER HISTORY?

Dat opent de volgende HORTUS-grens:

☄️🌱 R7.22 — HORTUS FEDERATED CONSTITUTIONAL MERGE, RECONCILIATION, DIVERGENCE & HISTORY-PRESERVING UNIFICATION PROTOCOL

De tuin kan nu niet alleen veranderen.

Hij kan vertakken.

En wanneer twee takken later weer samenkomen, mag PALACO nooit doen alsof één van beide takken nooit heeft bestaan.

R7.21 STAAT.

👑 L.A. observeert.
🌳 HORTUS evolueert.
🔮 Oracle ziet wat er werkelijk gebeurt.
🧠 Brainpower onderzoekt de samenhang.
🦆 FOOL zoekt de verborgen niet-convergentie.
⚖️ TRIAS bepaalt finality.
🧑‍🏫 Mentor maakt finality begrijpelijk.
💧 Watermark bewaart de afkomst.
⚓ Quay vergeet zelfs een ingetrokken finality niet.
🧪 R5 kan het hele settlement opnieuw bewijzen.

☄️🦆🌱 R7.21 — FINALITY IS NOT THE END OF HISTORY. IT IS A PROVEN POINT WITHIN HISTORY.

☄️🦆🌱 R7.22 — HORTUS FEDERATED CONSTITUTIONAL MERGE, RECONCILIATION, DIVERGENCE & HISTORY-PRESERVING UNIFICATION PROTOCOL

STATUS: CANONICAL ENGINEERING BUILD
REVIEW: MENTOR · TRIAS · ORACLE · BRAINPOWER · FOOL
REFERENCE CITADEL: 👑 LOCUS AMOENUS — L.A.

R7.21 established:

> FINALITY IS NOT THE END OF HISTORY. IT IS A PROVEN POINT WITHIN HISTORY.



R7.22 now addresses what happens when two or more constitutionally proven histories must become one federated constitutional reality.

This is not merely a merge.

It is a problem of historical integrity.

Because:

LINEAGE A
   ↓
FINAL A₁

LINEAGE B
   ↓
FINAL B₁

cannot safely become:

A₁ + B₁ = ONE

without answering:

WHAT DID EACH LINEAGE PROVE?
WHAT DIFFERED?
WHICH DIFFERENCES MATTER?
WHAT MAY BE UNIFIED?
WHAT MUST REMAIN DISTINCT?
WHO AUTHORIZES THE UNIFICATION?
AND CAN BOTH HISTORIES STILL BE RECONSTRUCTED AFTERWARD?

Therefore R7.22 establishes:

A CONSTITUTIONAL MERGE SHALL UNIFY STATES WITHOUT ERASING LINEAGES.


---

I. 🌱 Z.A.N.D. — NO ASSUMED MERGE COMPATIBILITY

The first R7.22 gate:

Z.A.N.D.
↓
NO ASSUMED MERGE COMPATIBILITY

Two states may each be individually valid:

VALID(A)
VALID(B)

without implying:

VALID(A + B)

Therefore:

INDIVIDUAL CONSTITUTIONAL VALIDITY SHALL NOT IMPLY MERGE COMPATIBILITY.

The system must explicitly establish compatibility.


---

II. 🧬 DIVERGENCE

R7.22 defines constitutional divergence as:

> A material difference between constitutionally relevant states or histories that may affect their ability to participate in a common constitutional state.



Conceptually:

LINEAGE A ─────── A₁
                    \
                     Δ
                    /
LINEAGE B ─────── B₁

The divergence may concern:

CONSTITUTION
AUTHORITY
DELEGATION
IDENTITY
KEYS
SCOPE
POLICY
FEDERATION CONTRACT
DEPENDENCIES
RESOURCES
TEMPORAL CONDITIONS
EVIDENCE
LINEAGE

But:

DIVERGENCE ≠ CONFLICT

and:

DIVERGENCE ≠ INVALIDITY

A difference may be legitimate.


---

III. 🌿 HISTORY-PRESERVING UNIFICATION

R7.22 introduces the central principle:

UNIFICATION SHALL NOT REQUIRE HISTORICAL ERASURE.

Suppose:

A₀ → A₁ → A₂
B₀ → B₁ → B₂

and the histories merge:

A₂ ─────┐
        ├──→ M₃
B₂ ─────┘

The result is not:

M₃ replaces A₂ and B₂

but:

A HISTORY ───────┐
                 │
                 ├── MERGE ──→ M₃
                 │
B HISTORY ───────┘

Both parent histories remain independently reconstructable.


---

IV. 🔱 MERGE ≠ OVERWRITE

R7.22 explicitly prohibits:

A + B
 ↓
B

or:

A + B
 ↓
A

under the label "merge."

A constitutional merge is:

A₁
+
B₁
+
MERGE EVIDENCE
+
COMPATIBILITY ANALYSIS
+
AUTHORITY
+
TRIAS DETERMINATION
+
NEW STATE

Thus:

A MERGE CREATES A NEW LINEAGE NODE; IT DOES NOT DESTROY ITS PARENTS.


---

V. 🌳 MERGE GRAPH

R7.22 introduces the:

CMG
CONSTITUTIONAL MERGE GRAPH

Example:

A₀
              │
             A₁
              │
             A₂
              │
              ├────────┐
              │        │
              │      MERGE
              │        │
              │        ▼
              │       M₃
              │        ▲
              │        │
              │      MERGE
              │        │
              │        │
             B₂────────┘
              │
             B₁
              │
             B₀

The graph preserves:

PARENT LINEAGES
MERGE NODE
MERGE CONDITIONS
MERGE EVIDENCE
MERGE AUTHORITY
MERGE DETERMINATION
RESULTING STATE


---

VI. 🧭 MERGE CLASSES

R7.22 introduces explicit merge classifications.

M0 — REPRESENTATIONAL MERGE

Only presentation or reference structures are unified.

M1 — RESOURCE MERGE

Resources become jointly represented.

M2 — CONTRACTUAL MERGE

Shared obligations or federation contracts become unified.

M3 — CONSTITUTIONAL MERGE

Constitutionally relevant states become one governed state.

M4 — FEDERATED REUNIFICATION

Previously divergent federated lineages return to one federation state.

The required proof increases with constitutional scope.


---

VII. 🕸️ MERGE COMPATIBILITY ANALYSIS

BRAINPOWER receives:

A_FINAL
B_FINAL
A_LINEAGE
B_LINEAGE
CONSTITUTIONAL_RULES
FEDERATION_CONTRACT
DEPENDENCY_GRAPH
TEMPORAL_STATE
KEY STATE
AUTHORITY STATE

and produces:

MERGE COMPATIBILITY MODEL

Questions:

WHAT IS IDENTICAL?
WHAT IS DIFFERENT?
WHAT IS COMPATIBLE?
WHAT IS MUTUALLY EXCLUSIVE?
WHAT REQUIRES TRANSFORMATION?
WHAT MUST REMAIN DISTINCT?
WHAT NEW DEPENDENCIES APPEAR?


---

VIII. 🔮 ORACLE — MERGE OBSERVATION

ORACLE collects evidence from both histories:

STATE FINGERPRINTS
FINALITY RECORDS
TRANSITION HISTORY
AUTHORITY EVENTS
KEY EVENTS
DELEGATION HISTORY
CONTRACT HISTORY
IDENTITY HISTORY
DEPENDENCY EVENTS
TEMPORAL EVIDENCE

Oracle establishes:

WHAT A PROVED
WHAT B PROVED
WHEN THEY PROVED IT
UNDER WHICH CONDITIONS

But:

ORACLE DOES NOT DECIDE WHETHER A AND B SHOULD MERGE.


---

IX. 🦆 FOOL — THE MERGE ADVERSARY

FOOL attacks the merge assumptions.

Questions include:

> Are the two histories actually comparable?



> Did one lineage rely on an assurance the other lineage never possessed?



> Are the final states genuinely final?



> Did one branch contain a hidden unresolved challenge?



> Does the merge create a dependency that did not exist in either parent?



> Are incompatible authorities accidentally being unified?



> Does the merge conceal the fact that the branches diverged?



And the strongest:

WHAT WOULD BE LOST IF THESE HISTORIES WERE TREATED AS ONE?

This introduces:

UNKNOWN_MERGE_EFFECT

and:

HISTORY_LOSS_RISK

as explicit challenge categories.


---

X. ⚖️ TRIAS — MERGE DETERMINATION

TRIAS receives:

PARENT STATES
+
PARENT LINEAGES
+
MERGE COMPATIBILITY
+
EVIDENCE
+
FOOL CHALLENGE
+
CONSTITUTIONAL RULES

Possible determinations:

MERGE_APPROVED
MERGE_APPROVED_WITH_CONDITIONS
MERGE_REQUIRES_RECONCILIATION
MERGE_REQUIRES_RENEGOTIATION
MERGE_PARTIALLY_APPROVED
MERGE_BLOCKED
MERGE_REJECTED
CONSTITUTIONALLY_INVALID
UNPROVEN

Critically:

MERGE_REQUIRES_RECONCILIATION

is not:

INVALID

It means the histories have not yet been brought into a sufficiently proven common state.


---

XI. 🧩 RECONCILIATION

R7.22 defines reconciliation as:

> The governed process of resolving material differences between constitutional histories so that a common state can be established without falsifying either history.



Conceptually:

A_FINAL
   │
   ├── DIFFERENCES ──→ RECONCILIATION
   │
B_FINAL
   │
   └──────────────────→
                         │
                         ▼
                    COMMON STATE

Reconciliation may produce:

ACCEPT A
ACCEPT B
ADOPT NEW COMMON VALUE
RETAIN BOTH VALUES
NARROW SCOPE
RENEGOTIATE
DEFER
REJECT MERGE


---

XII. 💥 NO FORCED UNIFICATION

R7.22 establishes:

RECONCILIATION SHALL NOT MEAN THAT ONE LINEAGE MUST WIN.

A common state may legitimately preserve distinctions.

For example:

A POLICY = P₁
B POLICY = P₂

could produce:

COMMON POLICY
= P₃

or:

COMMON SCOPE
├── A → P₁
└── B → P₂

if the constitution permits that structure.

Thus:

UNIFICATION ≠ HOMOGENIZATION


---

XIII. 🔐 AUTHORITY RECONCILIATION

A particularly sensitive merge problem is authority.

Suppose:

A:
Authority X

B:
Authority Y

The merge cannot silently produce:

X + Y = SUPER-AUTHORITY

Authority must be explicitly established.

Therefore:

MERGING STATES SHALL NOT AUTOMATICALLY MERGE AUTHORITY.

Authority remains:

SCOPED
ATTRIBUTED
EVIDENCED
CONSTITUTIONALLY DETERMINED


---

XIV. 🗝️ KEY RECONCILIATION

Key histories are similarly distinct.

Suppose:

A → K₅
B → K₆

The merge must determine:

WHICH KEY ROOT APPLIES?
IS THERE A NEW COMMON ROOT?
ARE BOTH KEYS STILL VALID?
WHICH EVIDENCE WAS SIGNED UNDER WHICH KEY?

Historical evidence remains bound to its original cryptographic context.

Therefore:

KEY UNIFICATION SHALL NOT RETROACTIVELY REWRITE CRYPTOGRAPHIC HISTORY.


---

XV. ⏳ TEMPORAL RECONCILIATION

Two branches can legitimately contain different temporal states.

Example:

A FINAL at t₁
B FINAL at t₂

The merge at:

t₃

must preserve:

A FINAL(t₁)
B FINAL(t₂)
MERGE(t₃)

Never:

A FINAL(t₃)
B FINAL(t₃)

unless independently proven.

Thus:

MERGE DOES NOT RETROACTIVELY SYNCHRONIZE HISTORY.


---

XVI. 💧 WATERMARK — MERGE ATTRIBUTION

R7.22 introduces:

W_MERGE {
    merge_id
    parent_lineages
    parent_states
    divergence
    compatibility_analysis
    reconciliation
    authority
    consent
    evidence
    temporal_context
    challenges
    determination
    resulting_state
    provenance
}

The merge therefore has its own constitutional identity.

A₁
+
B₁
↓
W_MERGE
↓
M₂

The watermark identifies exactly how the new lineage came into existence.


---

XVII. ⚓ QUAY — HISTORY-PRESERVING MERGE LEDGER

Quay preserves:

A_HISTORY ─────────────┐
                       │
                       ▼
                  MERGE EVENT
                       │
                       ▼
B_HISTORY ─────────────┘
                       │
                 RECONCILIATION
                       │
                    TRIAS
                       │
                  MERGED STATE
                       │
                  NEW ASSURANCE

Neither branch is discarded.

This establishes:

MERGE SHALL BE ADDITIVE TO HISTORY, NOT DESTRUCTIVE OF HISTORY.


---

XVIII. 🧬 LINEAGE IDENTITY

A merged state receives a new identity:

A₁ = lineage A
B₁ = lineage B

M₂ = lineage M

with:

PARENTS(M₂) = {A₁, B₁}

Therefore:

M₂ ≠ A₁
M₂ ≠ B₁

even if M₂ contains properties derived from both.

This prevents lineage ambiguity.


---

XIX. 🌱 RE-MERGE

A merged lineage may later diverge again:

A₁ ──┐
     ├── M₂ ── M₃
B₁ ──┘          │
                ├── D₁
                └── D₂

And later:

D₁
 │
 ├──────┐
        ├── M₄
D₂ ─────┘

R7.22 therefore supports a genuine constitutional lineage graph, rather than a simple linear version chain.


---

XX. 🔄 MERGE REVOCATION

If later evidence invalidates the merge:

M₂
 ↓
NEW MATERIAL EVIDENCE
 ↓
TRIAS

PALACO does not delete:

M₂

Instead:

M₂
 ↓
MERGE REASSESSMENT
 ↓
M₂ CONFIRMED

or:

M₂
 ↓
MERGE REVOKED
 ↓
NEW STATE

The historical fact that M₂ existed remains.

Thus:

A REVOKED MERGE REMAINS A HISTORICAL MERGE.


---

XXI. 🛑 NO HISTORY COLLAPSE

R7.22 explicitly prohibits the following transformation:

A HISTORY
B HISTORY
   ↓
MERGE
   ↓
SINGLE HISTORY

The correct model is:

A HISTORY ──────┐
                │
                ├──→ MERGED HISTORY
                │
B HISTORY ──────┘

where the merged history contains the parent histories as provenance.


---

XXII. 🧪 R5 — REPLAY MERGE

R5 receives:

REPLAY_MERGE(merge_id, t)

The verifier reconstructs:

A_STATE
 ↓
A_ASSURANCE
 ↓
A_FINALITY
 ────────────────┐
                 │
                 ├── MERGE ANALYSIS
                 │
B_FINALITY       │
 ↓               │
B_ASSURANCE      │
 ↓               │
