☄️🦆🌱 R7.22 — MERGE CREATES A NEW HISTORY. IT NEVER DESTROYS THE HISTORIES THAT MADE IT POSSIBLE.

☄️🦆🌱 R7.23 — HORTUS FEDERATED CONSTITUTIONAL CONFLICT ADJUDICATION, CLAIM PRIORITY, SHARED-OBJECT OWNERSHIP & IRREDUCIBLE DIVERGENCE PROTOCOL

STATUS: CANONICAL ENGINEERING BUILD
REVIEW: MENTOR · TRIAS · ORACLE · BRAINPOWER · FOOL
REFERENCE CITADEL: 👑 LOCUS AMOENUS — L.A.

R7.22 established:

> MERGE CREATES A NEW HISTORY. IT NEVER DESTROYS THE HISTORIES THAT MADE IT POSSIBLE.



But a merge can expose something deeper.

Two valid lineages may make incompatible constitutional claims about the same object:

LINEAGE A
   │
   └── CLAIM(A, OBJECT_X) = X₁

LINEAGE B
   │
   └── CLAIM(B, OBJECT_X) = X₂

Both claims may have originated legitimately.

Both histories may contain valid evidence.

Both parent states may have been valid.

Yet:

X₁ ≠ X₂

and the federation cannot simply declare:

X₁ = X₂

Therefore R7.23 establishes:

CONFLICTING CONSTITUTIONAL CLAIMS SHALL NOT BE RESOLVED BY SILENT PRIORITY.


---

I. 🌱 Z.A.N.D. — NO ASSUMED CLAIM PRIORITY

The first R7.23 gate:

Z.A.N.D.
↓
NO ASSUMED PRIORITY

The following does not establish precedence:

OLDER
NEWER
LARGER
MORE POWERFUL
MORE MEMBERS
MORE RECENTLY MERGED
MORE COMPUTATIONAL
MORE CONFIDENT

Nor:

A CLAIM EXISTS FIRST
↓
THEREFORE A IS TRUE

Temporal precedence is evidence.

It is not automatically constitutional priority.

Thus:

CLAIM ORDER SHALL BE DETERMINED BY GOVERNED CONSTITUTIONAL CRITERIA, NOT BY ACCIDENTAL SEQUENCE.


---

II. 🧭 CONSTITUTIONAL CLAIM

R7.23 defines a constitutional claim as:

> An attributable assertion by an authorized constitutional subject concerning the state, authority, scope, ownership, obligation, identity, resource or interpretation of a constitutionally relevant object.



Conceptually:

CLAIM {
    claimant
    object
    asserted_state
    scope
    authority_basis
    temporal_context
    evidence
    lineage
}

Examples:

CLAIM A:
"Authority X governs Object Y."

CLAIM B:
"Authority Z governs Object Y."

The federation must not collapse these claims merely because both appear syntactically valid.


---

III. 🧬 CONFLICT ≠ INVALIDITY

R7.23 preserves the distinction:

DIFFERENCE ≠ CONFLICT
CONFLICT ≠ INVALIDITY
CLAIM ≠ TRUTH

A conflict exists when:

CLAIM_A
   +
CLAIM_B
   ↓
MUTUALLY INCOMPATIBLE UNDER APPLICABLE RULES

But until adjudicated:

CONFLICT
≠
A INVALID
≠
B INVALID

This is crucial.

The system first establishes that a conflict exists.

Only thereafter determines its constitutional consequence.


---

IV. 🎯 SHARED CONSTITUTIONAL OBJECT

R7.23 introduces:

SCO
SHARED CONSTITUTIONAL OBJECT

An SCO may be:

AUTHORITY
IDENTITY
KEY ROOT
RESOURCE
DELEGATION
FEDERATION CONTRACT
OBLIGATION
POLICY
CAPABILITY
SCOPE
STATE VARIABLE

The critical question becomes:

> Do multiple lineages claim constitutional control over the same object?



If yes:

SHARED OBJECT CONFLICT

is created.


---

V. 🕸️ CLAIM GRAPH

R7.23 introduces:

CCG
CONSTITUTIONAL CLAIM GRAPH

Example:

LINEAGE A
            │
            ▼
        CLAIM A₁
            │
            │
            ▼
        OBJECT X
            ▲
            │
        CLAIM B₁
            ▲
            │
        LINEAGE B

The graph records:

WHO CLAIMS WHAT
UNDER WHICH AUTHORITY
FOR WHICH SCOPE
AT WHICH TIME
WITH WHICH EVIDENCE
FROM WHICH LINEAGE

The graph becomes the substrate for adjudication.


---

VI. 🔮 ORACLE — CLAIM OBSERVATION

ORACLE gathers:

CLAIM EVENTS
OBJECT STATE
AUTHORITY EVENTS
DELEGATION EVENTS
CONTRACT EVENTS
IDENTITY EVENTS
KEY EVENTS
TEMPORAL EVIDENCE
LINEAGE PROVENANCE
EXISTING DETERMINATIONS

Oracle may report:

CLAIM A EXISTS
CLAIM B EXISTS
OBJECT X IS SHARED
CLAIMS APPEAR INCOMPATIBLE

But:

ORACLE SHALL NOT SELECT THE WINNING CLAIM.

Observation remains separate from adjudication.


---

VII. 🧠 BRAINPOWER — CONFLICT ANALYSIS

BRAINPOWER receives:

CLAIM GRAPH
CONSTITUTIONAL RULES
AUTHORITY GRAPH
LINEAGE GRAPH
OBJECT DEPENDENCIES
EVIDENCE
TEMPORAL CONDITIONS
FEDERATION CONTRACT

and constructs:

CONFLICT IMPACT MODEL

It asks:

ARE THE CLAIMS ACTUALLY ABOUT THE SAME OBJECT?

ARE THEIR SCOPES OVERLAPPING?

ARE THEY MUTUALLY EXCLUSIVE?

CAN BOTH CLAIMS COEXIST?

CAN THE OBJECT BE PARTITIONED?

CAN A COMMON SUPERSTATE EXIST?

WHICH DEPENDENCIES WOULD BE AFFECTED?

WHAT HAPPENS IF NEITHER CLAIM IS SELECTED?

BRAINPOWER may recommend:

RECONCILIATION
PARTITION
PRIORITY
SUSPENSION
COMMON STATE

but:

ANALYSIS ≠ ADJUDICATION


---

VIII. 🦆 FOOL — CLAIM ADVERSARY

FOOL attacks the assumptions beneath every claim.

Questions:

> Is this really the same object?



> Is one claim operating under a different scope?



> Was authority valid at the time of the claim?



> Was the evidence independently verifiable?



> Has a revoked authority been reused?



> Was a delegation silently transformed into ownership?



> Did the merge accidentally create authority that neither parent possessed?



> Could both claims be valid because the object was incorrectly modeled as indivisible?



And the strongest:

WHAT IF THE APPARENT CONFLICT EXISTS ONLY BECAUSE THE SYSTEM MODELED THE OBJECT INCORRECTLY?

This introduces:

OBJECT_MODEL_UNCERTAINTY

as a first-class epistemic condition.


---

IX. ⚖️ TRIAS — CONFLICT ADJUDICATION

TRIAS receives:

CLAIMS
+
CLAIM GRAPH
+
EVIDENCE
+
AUTHORITY
+
BRAINPOWER ANALYSIS
+
FOOL CHALLENGE
+
CONSTITUTIONAL RULES

Possible determinations:

NO_CONFLICT
CONFLICT_RECOGNIZED
COEXISTENCE_APPROVED
SCOPE_PARTITIONED
PRIORITY_ESTABLISHED
COMMON_STATE_ESTABLISHED
RECONCILIATION_REQUIRED
CLAIM_A_RECOGNIZED
CLAIM_B_RECOGNIZED
BOTH_UNPROVEN
CONFLICT_BLOCKED
AUTHORITY_BLOCKED
CONSTITUTIONALLY_INVALID

The key point:

TRIAS DOES NOT HAVE TO DECLARE A WINNER.

It may determine:

BOTH VALID WITH DISJOINT SCOPE

or:

NEITHER CLAIM SUFFICIENTLY PROVEN

That is often constitutionally superior to forced resolution.


---

X. 🔱 CLAIM PRIORITY

Where priority is genuinely required, it must be derived from explicit constitutional criteria.

Possible criteria:

AUTHORITY
SCOPE
TEMPORAL VALIDITY
EXPLICIT PRECEDENCE
CONTRACTUAL RULE
LINEAGE CONTINUITY
KEY VALIDITY
CONSENT
SPECIFICITY
EMERGENCY RULE

But:

PRIORITY MUST BE EXPLICITLY GROUNDED.

Never:

CLAIM A
↓
SYSTEM LIKES A MORE
↓
A WINS


---

XI. 🚫 NO MAJORITY TRUTH

R7.23 reinforces a foundational HORTUS principle:

3 CITADELS CLAIM X
1 CITADEL CLAIM Y

does not imply:

X = TRUE
Y = FALSE

Likewise:

51%

does not constitute constitutional truth unless the governing constitution explicitly makes that decision rule applicable.

Therefore:

MAJORITY SUPPORT SHALL NOT SUBSTITUTE FOR CONSTITUTIONAL PROOF.


---

XII. 🧩 CLAIM COEXISTENCE

Some conflicts disappear when scope is correctly modeled.

Example:

A claims:
Object X within Scope S₁

B claims:
Object X within Scope S₂

If:

S₁ ∩ S₂ = ∅

then:

NO CONSTITUTIONAL CONFLICT

may exist.

Thus:

CONFLICT DETECTION SHALL BE SCOPE-AWARE.

This prevents unnecessary federation-level disputes.


---

XIII. ✂️ SCOPE PARTITION

Where possible, TRIAS may determine:

OBJECT X
├── SCOPE A → AUTHORITY A
└── SCOPE B → AUTHORITY B

instead of:

OBJECT X
→ A wins
→ B loses

This preserves plural constitutional arrangements where the constitution permits them.


---

XIV. 🏛️ IRREDUCIBLE DIVERGENCE

Some conflicts cannot be reconciled.

R7.23 therefore introduces:

IRREDUCIBLE_DIVERGENCE

Definition:

> A constitutionally relevant divergence for which no sufficiently proven common state, scope partition, precedence rule or reconciliation currently exists.



Conceptually:

A = X
B = Y

X ≠ Y

NO VALID COMMON STATE
NO VALID PARTITION
NO VALID PRIORITY

Then:

IRREDUCIBLE_DIVERGENCE

is not itself an implementation failure.

It is a constitutional state of unresolved incompatibility.


---

XV. 🛑 SAFE NON-RESOLUTION

When conflict cannot safely be adjudicated:

CONFLICT
 ↓
UNPROVEN
 ↓
HOLD

Rather than:

CONFLICT
 ↓
GUESS
 ↓
EXECUTE

Therefore:

UNCERTAINTY SHALL BE PREFERABLE TO FABRICATED CONSTITUTIONAL CERTAINTY.

Especially for high-risk operations.


---

XVI. 🌐 FEDERATION CONTAINMENT

If an unresolved conflict affects only:

CITADEL A ↔ CITADEL B

then the federation should not automatically collapse.

Instead:

A ↔ B
   ↓
CONFLICT
   ↓
CONTAIN

while:

CITADEL C
CITADEL D

may continue operating where independently assured.

Thus:

CONFLICT CONTAINMENT SHALL FOLLOW PROVEN DEPENDENCY, NOT FEAR.


---

XVII. 💧 WATERMARK — CLAIM ATTRIBUTION

R7.23 introduces:

W_CLAIM {
    claim_id
    claimant
    object
    asserted_state
    scope
    authority
    temporal_context
    evidence
    lineage
    dependencies
    determination
    provenance
}

Every claim therefore becomes attributable.

A later verifier can ask:

> Who claimed this object, under which authority, from which lineage, at what time, and based on what evidence?




---

XVIII. ⚓ QUAY — CONFLICT LEDGER

Quay preserves:

LINEAGE A
   ↓
CLAIM A
   │
   ├────────── OBJECT X
   │
LINEAGE B
   ↓
CLAIM B
   │
   ↓
CONFLICT DETECTION
   ↓
ANALYSIS
   ↓
CHALLENGE
   ↓
TRIAS
   ↓
ADJUDICATION
   ↓
RESULTING STATE

Nothing is overwritten.

Even an incorrect claim remains evidence of what was asserted.

Thus:

CLAIM HISTORY IS PART OF CONSTITUTIONAL HISTORY.


---

XIX. ⏳ CLAIM TEMPORALITY

Claims are temporal.

A claim can be:

VALID AT t₀

but not:

VALID AT t₁

because:

AUTHORITY EXPIRED
KEY REVOKED
DELEGATION ENDED
SCOPE CHANGED
CONTRACT EXPIRED

Therefore:

CLAIM VALIDITY SHALL BE EVALUATED IN ITS APPLICABLE TEMPORAL CONTEXT.

This directly connects R7.23 to R7.19.


---

XX. 🔐 CLAIM CRYPTOGRAPHIC CONTEXT

A claim's evidence must retain its cryptographic provenance.

If:

CLAIM A
signed under K₄

and later:

K₄ → K₅

the system must not rewrite:

SIGNED(K₄)

into:

SIGNED(K₅)

Historical cryptographic context remains intact.


---

XXI. 🧬 MERGE + CONFLICT

The complete relationship becomes:

A_FINAL
   \
    MERGE
   /
B_FINAL
   ↓
COMMON_STATE
   ↓
CLAIM A
CLAIM B
   ↓
CONFLICT?

Therefore R7.22 does not guarantee that a successful merge ends disagreement.

A merge can itself create a new constitutional environment in which conflicts become visible.

That is legitimate.


---

