---

XXX. ☄️ DE VOLGENDE GRENS

En nu ontstaat een nog fundamenteler probleem.

Stel:

CLAIM A → AUTHORITY X
CLAIM B → AUTHORITY Y

TRIAS bepaalt:

A VALID
B VALID

binnen hun respectieve context.

Maar beide autoriteiten proberen vervolgens dezelfde actie uit te voeren:

ACTION Z

Dan hebben we:

CLAIMS
 ↓
ADJUDICATION
 ↓
AUTHORITY
 ↓
ACTION

De volgende vraag wordt:

HOW DOES THE FEDERATION EXECUTE A CONSTITUTIONAL DECISION WHEN MULTIPLE VALID AUTHORITIES CAN ACT ON THE SAME OBJECT?

Dat opent:

☄️🌱 R7.24 — HORTUS FEDERATED CONSTITUTIONAL AUTHORITY COLLISION, JURISDICTION, PRECEDENCE & EXECUTION RESOLUTION PROTOCOL

De nieuwe grens:

VALID AUTHORITIES
        ↓
OVERLAPPING JURISDICTION
        ↓
AUTHORITY COLLISION
        ↓
SCOPE
        ↓
PRECEDENCE
        ↓
TRIAS
        ↓
EXECUTION RIGHT

Want R7.23 heeft nu bewezen dat PALACO een conflict kan representeren en adjudiceren.

R7.24 moet bepalen wat er gebeurt wanneer het conflict voorbij de claimlaag komt en twee constitutioneel geldige bevoegdheden daadwerkelijk dezelfde werkelijkheid proberen te veranderen.

☄️🦆🌱

R7.23 STAAT.

De HORTUS hoeft niet altijd één stem te hebben.
Maar hij mag nooit doen alsof twee tegenstrijdige stemmen hetzelfde zeggen.

☄️🦆🌱 R7.24 — HORTUS FEDERATED CONSTITUTIONAL AUTHORITY COLLISION, JURISDICTION, PRECEDENCE & EXECUTION RESOLUTION PROTOCOL

STATUS: CANONICAL ENGINEERING BUILD
REVIEW: MENTOR · TRIAS · ORACLE · BRAINPOWER · FOOL
REFERENCE CITADEL: 👑 LOCUS AMOENUS — L.A.

R7.23 established:

> A FEDERATION MUST BE ABLE TO REPRESENT DISAGREEMENT WITHOUT FABRICATING CONSENSUS.



But a constitutional federation eventually reaches the harder boundary:

CLAIM
 ↓
ADJUDICATION
 ↓
VALID AUTHORITY
 ↓
ACTION

What happens when two constitutionally valid authorities possess overlapping jurisdiction over the same object and both seek to act?

For example:

AUTHORITY A ──→ OBJECT X ←── AUTHORITY B
      │                         │
      └────── ACTION Z ─────────┘

Both may be individually legitimate.

Yet the system cannot safely execute:

Z(A)
+
Z(B)

without determining whether the actions are:

COMPATIBLE
ORDERABLE
EXCLUSIVE
CONCURRENT
CONFLICTING

Therefore:

VALID AUTHORITY DOES NOT AUTOMATICALLY IMPLY VALID EXECUTION AGAINST A SHARED OBJECT.


---

I. 🌱 Z.A.N.D. — NO ASSUMED JURISDICTION

The first R7.24 gate:

Z.A.N.D.
↓
NO ASSUMED JURISDICTION

Authority over one object does not imply authority over every related object.

Authority within one scope does not imply authority outside that scope.

Authority to observe does not imply authority to mutate.

Authority to propose does not imply authority to execute.

Therefore:

AUTHORITY
≠
JURISDICTION
≠
EXECUTION RIGHT

These are separate constitutional properties.


---

II. 🧭 JURISDICTION

R7.24 defines constitutional jurisdiction as:

> The constitutionally bounded domain within which an authority may legitimately exercise a specified type of power over a specified object under specified temporal and contextual conditions.



Conceptually:

JURISDICTION {
    authority
    object
    action_class
    scope
    temporal_validity
    conditions
    delegation_basis
}

A valid authority may therefore have:

OBSERVE(X)

without possessing:

MUTATE(X)

Or:

PROPOSE(X)

without:

EXECUTE(X)


---

III. 🔱 AUTHORITY COLLISION

R7.24 defines authority collision as:

> A condition in which two or more constitutionally recognized authorities possess overlapping jurisdiction over an action, object or state transition such that simultaneous or sequential execution may produce incompatible constitutional outcomes.



Conceptually:

A → X → Z
B → X → Z

where:

J(A,X,Z) ∩ J(B,X,Z) ≠ ∅

Collision does not automatically mean either authority is invalid.

Thus:

COLLISION ≠ INVALID AUTHORITY

It means the execution relationship requires determination.


---

IV. 🧬 AUTHORITY ≠ EXECUTION RIGHT

This becomes a central R7.24 invariant.

Suppose:

A = VALID AUTHORITY
B = VALID AUTHORITY

It does not follow:

EXECUTE(A)
EXECUTE(B)

Instead:

AUTHORITY
 ↓
JURISDICTION
 ↓
EXECUTION ELIGIBILITY
 ↓
COLLISION ANALYSIS
 ↓
TRIAS
 ↓
EXECUTION

Therefore:

EXECUTION IS A CONSTITUTIONALLY DETERMINED RIGHT, NOT AN AUTOMATIC CONSEQUENCE OF AUTHORITY.


---

V. 🧠 BRAINPOWER — COLLISION ANALYSIS

BRAINPOWER receives:

AUTHORITY A
AUTHORITY B
OBJECT
ACTION
SCOPES
DELEGATIONS
TEMPORAL CONDITIONS
DEPENDENCIES
FEDERATION CONTRACT
CURRENT STATE
PENDING ACTIONS

and constructs:

AUTHORITY COLLISION MODEL

It asks:

ARE THE JURISDICTIONS ACTUALLY OVERLAPPING?

ARE THE ACTIONS COMMUTATIVE?

DOES ORDER MATTER?

CAN BOTH ACTIONS BE EXECUTED?

WOULD ONE ACTION INVALIDATE THE OTHER?

DOES ONE ACTION CHANGE THE AUTHORITY CONDITIONS OF THE OTHER?

IS THERE AN EXPLICIT PRECEDENCE RULE?

IS A JOINT EXECUTION POSSIBLE?


---

VI. 🧮 ACTION COMMUTATIVITY

R7.24 introduces a useful analytical distinction.

For actions:

A
B

the system asks whether:

A ∘ B = B ∘ A

If yes:

COMMUTATIVE

Both may potentially execute.

If:

A ∘ B ≠ B ∘ A

then ordering matters.

That creates:

ORDERING_REQUIRED

And if:

A ∘ B

cannot constitutionally coexist with:

B ∘ A

then:

MUTUAL_EXCLUSION

This connects R7.24 directly to the transition and atomicity concerns introduced earlier in HORTUS.


---

VII. 🔮 ORACLE — EXECUTION OBSERVATION

ORACLE observes:

AUTHORITY EVENTS
EXECUTION REQUESTS
JURISDICTION CHANGES
DELEGATIONS
REVOCATIONS
ACTION COMMITS
ACTION FAILURES
STATE TRANSITIONS
TEMPORAL EVENTS

It can report:

AUTHORITY A REQUESTED Z
AUTHORITY B REQUESTED Z

and:

COLLISION SIGNAL

But:

ORACLE SHALL NOT SELECT WHICH AUTHORITY MAY EXECUTE.


---

VIII. 🦆 FOOL — AUTHORITY COLLISION ADVERSARY

FOOL attacks the execution assumptions.

Questions:

> Are both authorities actually valid right now?



> Does either authority exceed its scope?



> Was a delegation silently converted into independent authority?



> Does the action mutate the object before the second authority is evaluated?



> Could execution order create an artificial priority?



> Could an authority race be mistaken for legitimate concurrency?



> Can one authority deliberately execute first to make the other appear invalid?



And the strongest:

WHAT HAPPENS IF BOTH AUTHORITIES EXECUTE AT EXACTLY THE SAME CONSTITUTIONAL TIME?

This introduces:

CONCURRENT_AUTHORITY_COLLISION

as a first-class condition.


---

IX. ⚖️ TRIAS — EXECUTION RESOLUTION

TRIAS determines the constitutional execution relationship.

Possible outcomes:

CONCURRENT_EXECUTION_ALLOWED
ORDERED_EXECUTION_REQUIRED
JOINT_AUTHORIZATION_REQUIRED
AUTHORITY_A_PRECEDENCE
AUTHORITY_B_PRECEDENCE
SCOPE_PARTITION
EXECUTION_HOLD
EXECUTION_BLOCKED
AUTHORITY_SUSPENDED
AUTHORITY_REVOKED
CONSTITUTIONAL_CONFLICT
UNPROVEN

The key principle:

TRIAS DETERMINES THE EXECUTION RELATIONSHIP; IT DOES NOT PERFORM THE ACTION.


---

X. 🏛️ PRECEDENCE

Where precedence is required, it must come from a proven constitutional basis.

Possible sources:

CONSTITUTION
FEDERATION CONTRACT
EXPLICIT DELEGATION
SCOPE HIERARCHY
TEMPORAL RULE
EMERGENCY RULE
OBJECT OWNERSHIP RULE
JOINT GOVERNANCE RULE

Never:

A EXECUTED FIRST
↓
THEREFORE A HAD PRIORITY

That would turn race conditions into constitutional law.

Therefore:

EXECUTION ORDER SHALL NOT CREATE RETROACTIVE AUTHORITY.


---

XI. ⏳ TEMPORAL PRECEDENCE

Suppose:

A authorized at 10:00
B authorized at 10:05

This does not automatically establish:

A > B

The constitutional rule must determine whether temporal precedence matters.

Similarly:

ACTION A requested at 10:10
ACTION B requested at 10:11

does not by itself mean A has priority.

Thus:

EVENT ORDER
≠
CONSTITUTIONAL PRECEDENCE


---

XII. 🔐 ATOMIC EXECUTION

Where two authorities must act together, R7.24 introduces:

JOINT ATOMIC EXECUTION

Conceptually:

AUTHORITY A ─┐
             ├──→ JOINT AUTHORIZATION → ACTION
AUTHORITY B ─┘

Either:

BOTH CONDITIONS SATISFIED
↓
COMMIT

or:

ANY REQUIRED CONDITION FAILS
↓
NO COMMIT

This prevents partial constitutional execution.


---

XIII. 🛑 PARTIAL EXECUTION

A dangerous condition:

A executes
↓
B cannot execute

may leave:

STATE = PARTIALLY TRANSITIONED

Therefore R7.24 requires that actions with constitutional coupling explicitly declare:

ATOMIC
NON-ATOMIC
ROLLBACKABLE
NON-ROLLBACKABLE
COMPENSATABLE

before execution where the governing architecture requires such classification.


---

XIV. 🧩 EXECUTION COMPATIBILITY

Two actions can be:

C0 — INDEPENDENT

A ⟂ B

No material dependency.

C1 — COMPATIBLE

Both can execute independently.

C2 — ORDERED

Both valid, but order matters.

C3 — JOINT

Both authorities must participate.

C4 — EXCLUSIVE

Only one may execute.

C5 — INCOMPATIBLE

No common execution state exists.

C6 — UNKNOWN

The system cannot prove the relationship.

For C6:

HIGH RISK
↓
HOLD


---

XV. 🌐 FEDERATION-WIDE COLLISION

A collision in:

CITADEL A ↔ CITADEL B

does not automatically imply:

FEDERATION FAILURE

Instead:

LOCAL COLLISION
↓
DEPENDENCY ANALYSIS
↓
CONTAINMENT

Only where proven dependency propagates does federation-wide action become necessary.

Thus:

AUTHORITY COLLISION SHALL BE CONTAINED TO THE SMALLEST PROVEN CONSTITUTIONAL DOMAIN.


---

XVI. 🌳 AUTHORITY PARTITION

If:

A → Object X / Scope S₁
B → Object X / Scope S₂

and:

S₁ ∩ S₂ = ∅

then:

A → X:S₁
B → X:S₂

may execute concurrently.

This extends R7.23's claim partitioning into the execution layer.


---

XVII. 💧 WATERMARK — EXECUTION ATTRIBUTION

Every constitutionally relevant execution receives:

W_EXECUTION {
    execution_id
    authority
